115
00:05:05,110 --> 00:05:07,527
So just a little bit
of personal background.

116
00:05:07,527 --> 00:05:09,360
I think most of the
speakers that you've had

117
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:12,840
are from NLP, which makes
sense because transformers

118
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:13,750
came from NLP.

119
00:05:13,750 --> 00:05:16,680
I'm a computer
vision researcher,

120
00:05:16,680 --> 00:05:19,660
and I've been in visual
generation for a few years now.

121
00:05:19,660 --> 00:05:23,380
And it hasn't always been
as popular as it is now.

122
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:28,140
So how I got into
this, I was sat

123
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:30,420
in this lecture hall in
the Engineering Department

124
00:05:30,420 --> 00:05:33,810
at Oxford in, what, the
second year of my PhD.

125
00:05:33,810 --> 00:05:36,840
And we were having a talk by
Professor Antonio Torralba

126
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:38,280
from MIT.

127
00:05:38,280 --> 00:05:41,812
And he was presenting this
work called GAN Dissection.

128
00:05:41,812 --> 00:05:43,270
Some of you may
have heard of GANs.

129
00:05:43,270 --> 00:05:45,687
The state of the art image
generation approach at the time

130
00:05:45,687 --> 00:05:48,280
were these generative
adversarial networks.

131
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:53,440
And in 2019, you could generate
a blurry face, or a blurry

132
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:57,160
kitchen, or a blurry bedroom.

133
00:05:57,160 --> 00:05:59,540
And that was
amazing at the time.

134
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:02,320
And this paper here
was showing that you

135
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:06,350
could activate or deactivate
certain neurons in the GAN.

136
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:08,920
And in doing so, you could
make certain concepts

137
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:10,870
appear or disappear.

138
00:06:10,870 --> 00:06:13,270
So these are the kind
of images that we

139
00:06:13,270 --> 00:06:15,010
were generating at the time.

140
00:06:15,010 --> 00:06:17,990
So this is like
a blurry kitchen.

141
00:06:17,990 --> 00:06:19,910
And this was near
state of the art.

142
00:06:19,910 --> 00:06:23,325
So you can see, the visual
concepts are pretty messed up.

143
00:06:23,325 --> 00:06:24,950
It doesn't make a
huge amount of sense.

144
00:06:24,950 --> 00:06:28,100
But I can't stress how
good this was at the time.

145
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:32,290
I'm sure a lot of you have seen
DALL-E, and Stable Diffusion,

146
00:06:32,290 --> 00:06:33,760
and so on.

147
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:35,240
Things were not
always this good.

148
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:38,000
So the point of the paper,
or at least in this example,

149
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,660
was they were showing that you
could activate certain neurons

150
00:06:40,660 --> 00:06:42,680
and make windows
appear in the kitchen.

151
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:45,980
You could imagine how your
kitchen would look with windows.

152
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:49,890
And the outcome was this.

153
00:06:49,890 --> 00:06:52,710
So some pretty janky
windows on the left.

154
00:06:52,710 --> 00:06:57,220
And my mind was completely
blown, as I can sense,

155
00:06:57,220 --> 00:07:00,540
all of yours are as well.

156
00:07:00,540 --> 00:07:01,660
This was amazing.

157
00:07:01,660 --> 00:07:04,950
So what they showed here
is not only that the model

158
00:07:04,950 --> 00:07:07,140
had learned a physically
plausible place

159
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:08,710
to put the windows.

160
00:07:08,710 --> 00:07:11,130
But importantly, they'd also
shown that the model had

161
00:07:11,130 --> 00:07:12,580
learned some notion of physics.

162
00:07:12,580 --> 00:07:15,600
The model had learned that if
you put windows on the left hand

163
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:17,980
side of the room, light
will come through them,

164
00:07:17,980 --> 00:07:20,470
and there'll be a reflection
off the marble countertop.

165
00:07:20,470 --> 00:07:21,970
And you can see that here.

166
00:07:21,970 --> 00:07:25,630
And the model had just learned
this by looking at images.

167
00:07:25,630 --> 00:07:28,470
So this had a really
profound impact on me

168
00:07:28,470 --> 00:07:30,010
as a young PhD student.

169
00:07:30,010 --> 00:07:35,340
And I've been in visual
generation ever since.

170
00:07:35,340 --> 00:07:40,630
So the body of the talk,
there's going to be five parts.

171
00:07:40,630 --> 00:07:42,990
I'll give an
overview of the model

172
00:07:42,990 --> 00:07:46,960
that we trained to talk about
architecture, data and training

173
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:48,430
recipe, results
and applications,

174
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:53,080
and a little discussion
on what is next.

175
00:07:53,080 --> 00:07:57,400
So first, I included a
bit of historical context

176
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:59,930
on movie gen and
video generation.

177
00:07:59,930 --> 00:08:02,780
Maybe some of you are quite new
to the video generation field.

178
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:06,370
The field is quite
new inherently.

179
00:08:06,370 --> 00:08:10,790
This snapshot is maybe
three years from 2024,

180
00:08:10,790 --> 00:08:12,170
back to around 2022.

181
00:08:12,170 --> 00:08:16,330
This is a century in
machine learning research.

182
00:08:16,330 --> 00:08:19,390
I haven't included
all of the works here.

183
00:08:19,390 --> 00:08:22,190
There are some very relevant,
important works here.

184
00:08:22,190 --> 00:08:24,860
I've just included a snapshot
to make a couple of points.

185
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:30,280
There have been two milestone
events in video generation.

186
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:32,980
The first was in 2022
when people started

187
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:35,200
using diffusion modeling.

188
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:37,610
This is when the whole community
started using diffusion.

189
00:08:37,610 --> 00:08:41,440
That was a big step up in
visual quality at that point.

190
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:44,039
And the second one was in 2024.

191
00:08:44,039 --> 00:08:46,300
And this is the point
of today's talk.

192
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:48,330
So before 2024,
people were using

193
00:08:48,330 --> 00:08:52,540
quite small-scale
specialized architectures.

194
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:56,430
I say small because the
definition of small and large

195
00:08:56,430 --> 00:08:59,923
has been moving all
over the place recently.

196
00:08:59,923 --> 00:09:01,590
But these were
specialized architectures

197
00:09:01,590 --> 00:09:06,760
for computer vision, things
like CNNs, units, and so on.

198
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:10,830
And then around 2024,
video generation

199
00:09:10,830 --> 00:09:14,140
boarded this architecture
unification set up.

200
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:16,510
So all of the machine
learning fields,

201
00:09:16,510 --> 00:09:19,380
we've seen people ditch
specialized architectures

202
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:22,150
and move towards this
simple transformer setup.

203
00:09:22,150 --> 00:09:24,870
The reason is because all
of these different fields

204
00:09:24,870 --> 00:09:28,590
are seeing the benefits of
efficiency and scalability

205
00:09:28,590 --> 00:09:30,280
by moving to these transformers.

206
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:33,900
So in 2024, the video
generation community

207
00:09:33,900 --> 00:09:34,930
started doing the same.

208
00:09:34,930 --> 00:09:38,675
And that's where Movie Gen comes
in, which I'll talk about today.

209
00:09:38,675 --> 00:09:41,620

210
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:44,050
So a quick overview
on what movie

211
00:09:44,050 --> 00:09:45,470
is before we get
into the details.

212
00:09:45,470 --> 00:09:49,090
So Movie Gen was a cast of
foundation models that generates

213
00:09:49,090 --> 00:09:52,750
high-quality 1080p HD videos
with different aspect ratios

214
00:09:52,750 --> 00:09:53,932
