215
00:09:53,932 --> 00:09:55,390
Today, I'm just
going to be talking

216
00:09:55,390 --> 00:09:57,250
about the text-to-video model.

217
00:09:57,250 --> 00:09:59,770
I'll show some fun examples
later of the other models

218
00:09:59,770 --> 00:10:02,230
that we trained.

219
00:10:02,230 --> 00:10:05,753
And again, like I've been
saying, the point of the paper

220
00:10:05,753 --> 00:10:08,170
was showing that scaling data,
training compute, and model

221
00:10:08,170 --> 00:10:10,960
parameters for a simple
transformer trained

222
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:12,140
with flow matching--

223
00:10:12,140 --> 00:10:13,420
I'll cover that later--

224
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:16,030
yielded state of
the art results.

225
00:10:16,030 --> 00:10:20,410
We also presented a few
innovations and simplifications

226
00:10:20,410 --> 00:10:21,370
along the way.

227
00:10:21,370 --> 00:10:25,600
So Movie Gen Video, the
model, was a $30 billion

228
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:29,170
parameter foundation model
for joint text-to-image

229
00:10:29,170 --> 00:10:31,570
and text-to-video generation.

230
00:10:31,570 --> 00:10:33,430
The model was
trained on the order

231
00:10:33,430 --> 00:10:36,255
