253
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:32,640
We're learning P of x.

254
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:34,910
The question here
is what should x be?

255
00:11:34,910 --> 00:11:38,630
We know that x is going to be
derived in some way from videos,

256
00:11:38,630 --> 00:11:41,340
but there's an open question
of how exactly do we do that?

257
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:44,560

258
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:47,247
So to motivate what
we ended up doing,

259
00:11:47,247 --> 00:11:49,330
I'm just going to talk a
bit about the differences

260
00:11:49,330 --> 00:11:53,140
between text and media.

261
00:11:53,140 --> 00:11:56,320
So let's look at some
text data, for example.

262
00:11:56,320 --> 00:12:01,060
And this piece of data, a
sentence, an image of a cat.

263
00:12:01,060 --> 00:12:05,500
What has happened when this
data sample has been created

264
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:08,590
is a human has put in a
huge amount of thought

265
00:12:08,590 --> 00:12:11,560
into compressing
what they're thinking

266
00:12:11,560 --> 00:12:16,250
into this very well-designed,
semantically rich language.

267
00:12:16,250 --> 00:12:17,750
It's very compressed.

268
00:12:17,750 --> 00:12:20,270
Every word packs a huge
amount of information.

269
00:12:20,270 --> 00:12:22,910
And it's also
inherently discrete.

270
00:12:22,910 --> 00:12:25,940
So in practice, when people are
training large language models,

271
00:12:25,940 --> 00:12:28,550
they can use a representation
that's quite close to this.

272
00:12:28,550 --> 00:12:32,050
So they might use
a simple tokenizer

273
00:12:32,050 --> 00:12:35,720
before feeding it
into the transformer.

274
00:12:35,720 --> 00:12:38,250
Media data is incredibly
different to this.

275
00:12:38,250 --> 00:12:40,650
So let's look at a
related piece of data,

276
00:12:40,650 --> 00:12:43,700
an actual image of a cat.

277
00:12:43,700 --> 00:12:46,830
No compression has
really occurred here.

278
00:12:46,830 --> 00:12:49,340
This image is just
continuous raw data that's

279
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:52,760
been captured by your camera.

280
00:12:52,760 --> 00:12:55,610
Maybe the only human effort
that's gone into this

281
00:12:55,610 --> 00:12:57,920
is positioning the camera
and framing the cat

282
00:12:57,920 --> 00:13:00,740
in the middle of the
camera, but nothing else.

283
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:03,950
As a result of this,
there's tons of redundancy.

284
00:13:03,950 --> 00:13:09,470
So what I mean by this is if
you what a cat is, and then,

285
00:13:09,470 --> 00:13:11,810
let's say, the
middle pixel you know

286
00:13:11,810 --> 00:13:13,647
is this white fur on the cat.

287
00:13:13,647 --> 00:13:15,147
Well, if you know
what a cat is, you

288
00:13:15,147 --> 00:13:16,970
know the next pixel
along is probably

289
00:13:16,970 --> 00:13:20,070
going to be white fur and
the next one along from that.

290
00:13:20,070 --> 00:13:22,490
And if it's a video, you
know the same pixels,

291
00:13:22,490 --> 00:13:25,320
one frame along, are also
going to be white cat fur.

292
00:13:25,320 --> 00:13:28,100
There's a huge
amount of redundancy.

293
00:13:28,100 --> 00:13:30,830
So this begs the question
of perhaps this raw data

294
00:13:30,830 --> 00:13:33,020
could be transformed
into something that more

295
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:36,730
closely resembles language.

296
00:13:36,730 --> 00:13:39,365
So what do we actually do?

297
00:13:39,365 --> 00:13:40,990
So if you forget
everything that I just

298
00:13:40,990 --> 00:13:44,080
said on the previous slide,
one thing you could do,

299
00:13:44,080 --> 00:13:47,830
a very simple approach, is you
just model the pixels directly.

300
00:13:47,830 --> 00:13:50,150
So let's say, you took
an image or a video,

301
00:13:50,150 --> 00:13:52,990
you unraveled it into a
long sequence of pixels,

302
00:13:52,990 --> 00:13:57,140
and then trained, let's say,
next token prediction on that.

303
00:13:57,140 --> 00:13:59,870
And in a way, that's what
some prior works did.

304
00:13:59,870 --> 00:14:02,620
Things like Imagen
Video or ImageGPT

305
00:14:02,620 --> 00:14:06,550
back in the day, along with
a little bit of patching.

306
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:11,720
So this is a very
conceptually simple method,

307
00:14:11,720 --> 00:14:14,060
but it's very
computationally constraining.

308
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:17,560
So the thing about
modeling pixels

309
00:14:17,560 --> 00:14:19,450
directly is the number
of pixels scales

310
00:14:19,450 --> 00:14:23,620
quadratically with the
image or video resolution.

311
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:24,740
It's bad for images.

312
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:27,790
It's even worse for video when
you have a temporal dimension.

313
00:14:27,790 --> 00:14:32,570
In practice, what this means is
these models can only model very

314
00:14:32,570 --> 00:14:36,960
low resolution images or videos,
things like 64-by-64 videos,

315
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:38,070
which is not ideal.

316
00:14:38,070 --> 00:14:42,360
And if we want to generate
a actual large HD video,

317
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:45,680
these methods had to employ
a huge cascade of upsampling

318
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:46,230
stages.

319
00:14:46,230 --> 00:14:50,060
Things super resolution models,
frame interpolation models

320
00:14:50,060 --> 00:14:52,290
to increase the
size of the data.

321
00:14:52,290 --> 00:14:55,850
So this really isn't ideal.

322
00:14:55,850 --> 00:14:58,550
So instead, what
prior work does is

323
00:14:58,550 --> 00:15:02,510
they learn a compressed latent
representation using something

324
00:15:02,510 --> 00:15:07,130
like a VAE or a VQ-VAE
trained offline.

325
00:15:07,130 --> 00:15:10,490
This is what every text
image model or text video

326
00:15:10,490 --> 00:15:12,080
model you've seen
on social media

327
00:15:12,080 --> 00:15:14,510
has been doing for a long time.

328
00:15:14,510 --> 00:15:17,570
The advantages here are twofold.

329
00:15:17,570 --> 00:15:20,430
If you're modeling a compressed
version of your data,

330
00:15:20,430 --> 00:15:23,010
then you can natively
model larger data.

331
00:15:23,010 --> 00:15:25,080
We don't need to go
down to 64-by-64.

332
00:15:25,080 --> 00:15:26,940
We can natively model
something bigger.

333
00:15:26,940 --> 00:15:30,250
The other advantage
is that this offline

334
00:15:30,250 --> 00:15:34,540
trained VAE or VQ-VAE can remove
some of the computational burden

335
00:15:34,540 --> 00:15:36,080
from the language model.

336
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:39,010
For example, these
autoencoders could

337
00:15:39,010 --> 00:15:42,850
handle the modeling of how
two separate blades of grass

338
00:15:42,850 --> 00:15:45,410
differ from each other
in an image or a video.

339
00:15:45,410 --> 00:15:48,810
And it can take that burden off
the downstream language model.

340
00:15:48,810 --> 00:15:51,380

341
00:15:51,380 --> 00:15:54,940
So that is what we do.

342
00:15:54,940 --> 00:15:57,640
From an architecture,
we train something

343
00:15:57,640 --> 00:16:02,170
called a temporal autoencoder
for spatial temporal video

344
00:16:02,170 --> 00:16:03,370
compression.

345
00:16:03,370 --> 00:16:07,390
This is basically just a
variational autoencoder.

346
00:16:07,390 --> 00:16:08,870
How does this work?

347
00:16:08,870 --> 00:16:13,460
Well, you take a video, you'll
feed it through the TAE encoder.

348
00:16:13,460 --> 00:16:16,510
A VAE consists of an
encoder and a decoder.

349
00:16:16,510 --> 00:16:19,833
After the encoder,
the representation

350
00:16:19,833 --> 00:16:21,250
will be compressed,
and you end up

351
00:16:21,250 --> 00:16:24,582
with this latent representation
at the bottleneck in the middle.

352
00:16:24,582 --> 00:16:26,290
And this is the
representation that we're

353
00:16:26,290 --> 00:16:29,420
going to use downstream.

354
00:16:29,420 --> 00:16:31,830
How you train these
things is quite simple.

355
00:16:31,830 --> 00:16:33,330
Some of you may
have seen it before.

356
00:16:33,330 --> 00:16:34,163
You take your video.

357
00:16:34,163 --> 00:16:35,550
You feed it through the encoder.

358
00:16:35,550 --> 00:16:38,400
You get to the bottleneck
latent representation.

359
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:40,830
You then decode it
back to pixel space.

360
00:16:40,830 --> 00:16:42,950
And you have a bunch of
losses between the output

361
00:16:42,950 --> 00:16:45,390
and the input, things
like L1 losses,

362
00:16:45,390 --> 00:16:48,710
adversarial losses, and so on.

363
00:16:48,710 --> 00:16:51,740
So this representation
in the middle

364
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:53,060
is what we're going to model.

365
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:54,770
When we talk about
learning P of x,

366
00:16:54,770 --> 00:16:58,790
we're learning the
distribution of this latent.

367
00:16:58,790 --> 00:17:02,010
What this means is when we train
a generative model on this,

368
00:17:02,010 --> 00:17:03,500
it generates in this space.

369
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:05,369
It doesn't generate
an RGB space.

370
00:17:05,369 --> 00:17:07,520
So after we generate
a video, we then

371
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:12,560
need to decode it
back to RGB space.

372
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:18,079
So the TAE that we trained
had 8x compression in each

373
00:17:18,079 --> 00:17:18,750
dimension--

374
00:17:18,750 --> 00:17:22,099
8x in height, width, and time.

375
00:17:22,099 --> 00:17:27,160
And this was pretty high
compression at the time.

376
00:17:27,160 --> 00:17:29,540
It's not the highest
compression anymore.

377
00:17:29,540 --> 00:17:31,760
This was published
like six months ago.

378
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:34,950
And six months is, again, like
a decade in machine learning

379
00:17:34,950 --> 00:17:35,450
research.

380
00:17:35,450 --> 00:17:37,450
But at the time, this was
very high compression.

381
00:17:37,450 --> 00:17:40,570
And like I said, this means
that we can natively model

382
00:17:40,570 --> 00:17:42,980
very high-resolution videos.

383
00:17:42,980 --> 00:17:48,430
As an example, the largest video
that we model in this work is

384
00:17:48,430 --> 00:17:54,010
768-by-768 pixels,
16 seconds, 16 FPS.

385
00:17:54,010 --> 00:17:57,940
Now, if we were to
model pixels directly,

386
00:17:57,940 --> 00:18:01,160
and we took a video of that
size, we unraveled everything,

387
00:18:01,160 --> 00:18:03,040
we treated one
pixel as one token,

388
00:18:03,040 --> 00:18:04,850
and we flattened
the whole thing,

389
00:18:04,850 --> 00:18:07,900
it would result in
150 million tokens.

390
00:18:07,900 --> 00:18:11,590
Even with very long
contact training methods

391
00:18:11,590 --> 00:18:12,470
with language models.

392
00:18:12,470 --> 00:18:15,220
At the moment, this is
completely unfeasible.

393
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:18,490
But using this
temporal autoencoder,

394
00:18:18,490 --> 00:18:23,320
the same video is just
compressed to 73,000 tokens.

395
00:18:23,320 --> 00:18:26,390
So this is suddenly completely
computationally feasible

396
00:18:26,390 --> 00:18:29,840
using today's
parallelism approaches,

397
00:18:29,840 --> 00:18:32,210
today's infrastructure.

398
00:18:32,210 --> 00:18:34,320
If anyone is doing
this maths offline,

399
00:18:34,320 --> 00:18:36,210
there is also a
patch of high layer--

400
00:18:36,210 --> 00:18:38,680
if someone thinks my
maths is wrong here.

401
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:42,360

402
00:18:42,360 --> 00:18:44,640
So that's everything
for the presentation.

403
00:18:44,640 --> 00:18:46,840
Does anyone have any
questions at this point?

404
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:49,695

405
00:18:49,695 --> 00:18:52,670
[INAUDIBLE]?

406
00:18:52,670 --> 00:18:54,980
No.

407
00:18:54,980 --> 00:18:57,260
It's a great point.

408
00:18:57,260 --> 00:19:04,490
A lot of autoencoders for
videos do use causality.

409
00:19:04,490 --> 00:19:07,820
Some nice outcomes
of that is that when

410
00:19:07,820 --> 00:19:12,320
you encode images that can be
encoded completely independently

411
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:14,700
of subsequent frames and so on.

412
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:15,910
But no, this isn't causal.

413
00:19:15,910 --> 00:19:18,800

414
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:23,620
So next up, which generative
modeling learning objective

415
00:19:23,620 --> 00:19:24,160
do we use?

416
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:26,980
So probably in most of the
talks that you've had so far,

417
00:19:26,980 --> 00:19:28,710
you've heard about
autoregression

418
00:19:28,710 --> 00:19:32,550
and next token
prediction for text.

419
00:19:32,550 --> 00:19:35,640
In media generation,
we haven't been doing

420
00:19:35,640 --> 00:19:37,000
that for a couple of years.

421
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:40,020
So the de facto approach
in most media generation

422
00:19:40,020 --> 00:19:41,890
has been using
diffusion modeling.

423
00:19:41,890 --> 00:19:44,855
We use something
called flow matching.

424
00:19:44,855 --> 00:19:49,860

425
00:19:49,860 --> 00:19:51,900
So what is flow matching?

426
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:56,610
Flow matching is, in a way,
a simpler generalization

427
00:19:56,610 --> 00:19:58,170
of diffusion.

428
00:19:58,170 --> 00:20:02,220
Now, if any of you have watched
a talk on diffusion modeling

429
00:20:02,220 --> 00:20:05,410
or read anything about
diffusion or flow matching,

430
00:20:05,410 --> 00:20:08,130
I'm sure you've seen a
figure like this before.

431
00:20:08,130 --> 00:20:11,550
I'm going to give a
brief explainer of what

432
00:20:11,550 --> 00:20:14,280
is similar between flow
matching and diffusion.

433
00:20:14,280 --> 00:20:17,170
And then we're going to go over
what a training step looks like.

434
00:20:17,170 --> 00:20:21,030
So both diffusion
and flow matching

435
00:20:21,030 --> 00:20:23,580
have a very similar
setup, so you

436
00:20:23,580 --> 00:20:26,470
assume that you have some
unknown data distribution.

437
00:20:26,470 --> 00:20:29,290
This is the distribution
of images in this figure.

438
00:20:29,290 --> 00:20:32,880
In this case, like
images of cats.

439
00:20:32,880 --> 00:20:34,938
This is the distribution
you're trying to learn.

440
00:20:34,938 --> 00:20:36,480
This is the distribution
that we want

441
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:38,790
to learn and then sample from.

442
00:20:38,790 --> 00:20:43,120
You also assume you have a known
data distribution on the right.

443
00:20:43,120 --> 00:20:47,970
And we model this as normally
just like Gaussian noise.

444
00:20:47,970 --> 00:20:51,880
Both assume then that we have
this fixed forward process.

445
00:20:51,880 --> 00:20:53,460
What this means is
we have a method

446
00:20:53,460 --> 00:20:57,210
of translating between
the unknown data

447
00:20:57,210 --> 00:21:01,920
distribution and the known one
by iteratively adding noise.

448
00:21:01,920 --> 00:21:04,120
Both assume that if you
keep on adding noise,

449
00:21:04,120 --> 00:21:08,220
you basically end up at this
known data distribution.

450
00:21:08,220 --> 00:21:11,160
Then, both of them
train a neural network

451
00:21:11,160 --> 00:21:12,790
to do the reverse process.

452
00:21:12,790 --> 00:21:16,230
So they train a neural network
to take one of these images

453
00:21:16,230 --> 00:21:18,070
and iteratively denoise it.

454
00:21:18,070 --> 00:21:20,717
And then at inference
time, you can iteratively

455
00:21:20,717 --> 00:21:22,800
use this neural network
to go from a sample that's

456
00:21:22,800 --> 00:21:26,400
pure noise back to a sample
from this data distribution

457
00:21:26,400 --> 00:21:27,580
that you've just learned.

458
00:21:27,580 --> 00:21:31,980
And that's how we end up
sampling images and videos.

459
00:21:31,980 --> 00:21:34,110
So diffusion and
flow matching are

460
00:21:34,110 --> 00:21:36,160
very similar in a lot of ways.

461
00:21:36,160 --> 00:21:39,840
Flow matching is, in a way,
a simpler generalization.

462
00:21:39,840 --> 00:21:42,330
It's been very recently
shown to result

463
00:21:42,330 --> 00:21:46,140
in more robust training and more
efficient probability paths that

464
00:21:46,140 --> 00:21:48,010
are easier and faster
to sample from.

465
00:21:48,010 --> 00:21:51,390
So this paper came out
pretty recently in 2023

466
00:21:51,390 --> 00:21:53,590
from some colleagues at Meta.

467
00:21:53,590 --> 00:21:56,460

468
00:21:56,460 --> 00:22:00,390
Importantly, it's been shown
to work better than diffusion.

469
00:22:00,390 --> 00:22:03,580
And I'm not going to go into
a huge amount of detail here,

470
00:22:03,580 --> 00:22:09,000
but we're going to go over how
a training step looks like.

471
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,710
So there are lots
of equations here.

472
00:22:11,710 --> 00:22:14,070
We're going to step
through them pretty easily.

473
00:22:14,070 --> 00:22:17,170
It's a three-step process.

474
00:22:17,170 --> 00:22:19,670
We first take a training
data sample, X1.

475
00:22:19,670 --> 00:22:23,150
This is your image of a
cat on the previous slide.

476
00:22:23,150 --> 00:22:25,750
This is just an image
from your data set.

477
00:22:25,750 --> 00:22:27,590
We then sample a time step.

478
00:22:27,590 --> 00:22:29,710
This is a float
between zero and one.

479
00:22:29,710 --> 00:22:32,360
And we sample from the
known data distribution.

480
00:22:32,360 --> 00:22:36,640
This just means taking a
sample from a normal Gaussian.

481
00:22:36,640 --> 00:22:40,300
We then construct a
training, sample Xt.

482
00:22:40,300 --> 00:22:41,240
What is this?

483
00:22:41,240 --> 00:22:47,350
This just intermediatte image,
a somewhat noisy image of a cat.

484
00:22:47,350 --> 00:22:50,920
There are lots of different
ways of constructing Xt.

485
00:22:50,920 --> 00:22:53,470
We use what's called the
simple linear interpolation

486
00:22:53,470 --> 00:22:55,640
from the flow matching paper.

487
00:22:55,640 --> 00:22:57,230
And the equation is shown here.

488
00:22:57,230 --> 00:22:59,200
So this is how we
go from the three

489
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:04,180
things we sampled above to this
intermediate training sample.

490
00:23:04,180 --> 00:23:05,650
Then, in flow
matching, what you do

491
00:23:05,650 --> 00:23:07,990
is you train the model
to predict the velocity.

492
00:23:07,990 --> 00:23:10,130
This is a value, which
moves the training,

493
00:23:10,130 --> 00:23:14,670
sample back in the direction
of the data sample.

494
00:23:14,670 --> 00:23:16,570
In actuality, it's very simple.

495
00:23:16,570 --> 00:23:18,780
So this is how we compute
the velocity, simply

496
00:23:18,780 --> 00:23:21,520
by differentiating
the equation above.

497
00:23:21,520 --> 00:23:23,470
This is how we get
our ground truth.

498
00:23:23,470 --> 00:23:26,440
And then on the right, we have
our actual learning objective.

499
00:23:26,440 --> 00:23:27,960
So this is the
mean squared error

500
00:23:27,960 --> 00:23:31,030
between the model prediction
and the ground truth velocity.

501
00:23:31,030 --> 00:23:35,440
Here, the model prediction
is parametrized by u.

502
00:23:35,440 --> 00:23:38,440
It takes as input
the training sample.

503
00:23:38,440 --> 00:23:40,650
It's conditioned on two things.

504
00:23:40,650 --> 00:23:43,410
It's conditioned on the text
prompt, that's P. Remember,

505
00:23:43,410 --> 00:23:45,070
we're doing
text-to-video generation.

506
00:23:45,070 --> 00:23:47,920
So we need to condition the
generation on the text prompt.

507
00:23:47,920 --> 00:23:50,220
I'll cover later how we do this.

508
00:23:50,220 --> 00:23:52,180
You condition on the
time step as well.

509
00:23:52,180 --> 00:23:57,090
And then theta are
the model parameters.

510
00:23:57,090 --> 00:24:01,170
You know know to
do flow matching.

511
00:24:01,170 --> 00:24:03,400
Inference is also pretty simple.

512
00:24:03,400 --> 00:24:06,330
So you start by sampling from
this known data distribution

513
00:24:06,330 --> 00:24:07,450
Gaussian noise.

514
00:24:07,450 --> 00:24:10,530
And then we use an ordinary
differential equation solver

515
00:24:10,530 --> 00:24:14,260
to go back to the data
distribution, given

516
00:24:14,260 --> 00:24:15,590
a series of time steps.

517
00:24:15,590 --> 00:24:18,290
So very simply, you'll
sample some noise.

518
00:24:18,290 --> 00:24:20,270
You'll sample a
stream of time steps.

519
00:24:20,270 --> 00:24:23,050
At each time step, you
compute the model's prediction

520
00:24:23,050 --> 00:24:24,050
for the velocity.

521
00:24:24,050 --> 00:24:26,350
And you use the solver
to move the sample

522
00:24:26,350 --> 00:24:29,830
in the direction of the
unknown data distribution.

523
00:24:29,830 --> 00:24:34,990
And at the end of that,
you have your sample.

524
00:24:34,990 --> 00:24:38,330
Low-level detail, we use
a quite simple solver.

525
00:24:38,330 --> 00:24:40,455
There are lots of different
options you can choose.

526
00:24:40,455 --> 00:24:43,570

527
00:24:43,570 --> 00:24:52,270
So lastly, which model
architecture do we use?

528
00:24:52,270 --> 00:24:55,930
Now, I already said that
we're using transformers.

529
00:24:55,930 --> 00:25:00,100
The big goal of this paper was
to benefit from, like I said,

530
00:25:00,100 --> 00:25:02,950
about seven times already,
scaling data, training compute,

531
00:25:02,950 --> 00:25:05,690
and model parameters
with transformers.

532
00:25:05,690 --> 00:25:08,710
But there is a question about
which transformer to use.

533
00:25:08,710 --> 00:25:13,200
So in my research
organization, we

534
00:25:13,200 --> 00:25:15,960
train these things
called Llamas.

535
00:25:15,960 --> 00:25:20,910
Llama is the large language
model that Meta open sources.

536
00:25:20,910 --> 00:25:25,450
We take the Llama 3 model, hence
why I pasted this L3 on it.

537
00:25:25,450 --> 00:25:29,640
Llama 3 is quite a classic
dense, fully connected,

538
00:25:29,640 --> 00:25:34,470
decoder-only language model.

539
00:25:34,470 --> 00:25:37,900
So what we did in Movie Gen
is, you take your videos,

540
00:25:37,900 --> 00:25:41,290
you encode them with the TAE
that we discussed earlier.

541
00:25:41,290 --> 00:25:43,240
You flatten the tokens.

542
00:25:43,240 --> 00:25:44,790
This gives you your
input sequence.

543
00:25:44,790 --> 00:25:47,460
And we just throw it into Llama.

544
00:25:47,460 --> 00:25:48,770
So very, very simple.

545
00:25:48,770 --> 00:25:52,050

546
00:25:52,050 --> 00:25:57,120
Now, when I say Llama I don't
mean a pre-trained Llama.

547
00:25:57,120 --> 00:26:01,530
I don't mean one that's
been trained for text.

548
00:26:01,530 --> 00:26:03,070
What I mean is the architecture.

549
00:26:03,070 --> 00:26:05,850
So a randomly
initialized architecture.

550
00:26:05,850 --> 00:26:08,530
But this is still
very important.

551
00:26:08,530 --> 00:26:11,140
Training large language
models at scale

552
00:26:11,140 --> 00:26:14,788
is incredibly
difficult. Every time

553
00:26:14,788 --> 00:26:16,580
you change anything
about the architecture,

554
00:26:16,580 --> 00:26:18,080
you need different
hyperparameters,

555
00:26:18,080 --> 00:26:19,280
they scale differently.

556
00:26:19,280 --> 00:26:21,050
It's incredibly tricky.

557
00:26:21,050 --> 00:26:25,870
So the fact that we already,
in our research organization,

558
00:26:25,870 --> 00:26:28,330
how to scale this
architecture, and the fact

559
00:26:28,330 --> 00:26:30,100
that we have the
infra set up already

560
00:26:30,100 --> 00:26:33,175
to train these things at
scale makes a huge difference.

561
00:26:33,175 --> 00:26:35,050
So that's why the simplest
thing for us to do

562
00:26:35,050 --> 00:26:37,690
was to go with the
Llama architecture.

563
00:26:37,690 --> 00:26:38,740
Question.

564
00:26:38,740 --> 00:26:44,950
So did you ever try taking an
initial or a pre-trained Llama?

565
00:26:44,950 --> 00:26:46,542
We didn't do that
for this project,

566
00:26:46,542 --> 00:26:48,750
but I agree that would be
a really cool thing to try.

567
00:26:48,750 --> 00:26:54,770

568
00:26:54,770 --> 00:27:00,080
It's not entirely clear
why that would work.

569
00:27:00,080 --> 00:27:03,350
Obviously, these are very
different modalities,

570
00:27:03,350 --> 00:27:05,900
different learning objectives.

571
00:27:05,900 --> 00:27:09,290
But in a lot of
ways, there's a lot

572
00:27:09,290 --> 00:27:11,930
of shared structure between
these modalities that

573
00:27:11,930 --> 00:27:14,250
would maybe benefit from that.

574
00:27:14,250 --> 00:27:17,510

575
00:27:17,510 --> 00:27:23,240
So that last slide was very
deceptively oversimplified.

576
00:27:23,240 --> 00:27:26,040
There are some changes that
we needed to make to Llama 3.

577
00:27:26,040 --> 00:27:29,510
So importantly, Llama 3 is a
model for autoregressive text

578
00:27:29,510 --> 00:27:30,390
generation.

579
00:27:30,390 --> 00:27:33,310
And we are doing text-to-video
generation using flow matching.

580
00:27:33,310 --> 00:27:35,310
So there are three changes
that we need to make.

581
00:27:35,310 --> 00:27:37,520
I'm going to go over
all of them exhaustively

582
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:43,580
to hammer the point that we
barely changed the architecture.

583
00:27:43,580 --> 00:27:45,140
The first thing
that we need to do

584
00:27:45,140 --> 00:27:46,917
is incorporate the
text conditioning.

585
00:27:46,917 --> 00:27:48,500
You'll have seen on
the previous slide

586
00:27:48,500 --> 00:27:51,480
that our input sequence is
just made up of video tokens.

587
00:27:51,480 --> 00:27:53,490
We're doing
text-to-video generation,

588
00:27:53,490 --> 00:27:56,640
so we need to incorporate the
text conditioning somehow.

589
00:27:56,640 --> 00:28:00,260
And we do this using
cross-attention layers.

590
00:28:00,260 --> 00:28:05,110
So very simply, we
construct a sequence made up

591
00:28:05,110 --> 00:28:08,140
of our text conditioning, and
we add cross-attention layers

592
00:28:08,140 --> 00:28:09,475
into the transformer block.

593
00:28:09,475 --> 00:28:11,350
So these go between the
self-attention layers

594
00:28:11,350 --> 00:28:14,080
and the feedforward network.

595
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:16,390
It's a very common way of
adding text conditioning

596
00:28:16,390 --> 00:28:18,000
for media generation models.

597
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:21,280

598
00:28:21,280 --> 00:28:23,800
There is a question as well
of what should your text

599
00:28:23,800 --> 00:28:25,250
representation be?

600
00:28:25,250 --> 00:28:27,470
How should you
construct this sequence?

601
00:28:27,470 --> 00:28:30,130
One very simple
thing to do would

602
00:28:30,130 --> 00:28:34,300
be you just tokenize the
prompt, the caption, and then

603
00:28:34,300 --> 00:28:35,360
you feed that in.

604
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:37,210
But when you do that,
you're very much

605
00:28:37,210 --> 00:28:39,910
burdening your model
with learning this text

606
00:28:39,910 --> 00:28:41,420
representation from scratch.

607
00:28:41,420 --> 00:28:46,060
So instead, we use pre-trained
text representations.

608
00:28:46,060 --> 00:28:48,400
We use three, in fact--

609
00:28:48,400 --> 00:28:50,510
three that are
complementary to each other.

610
00:28:50,510 --> 00:28:54,170
The first two-- all of them are
pre-trained frozen text models

611
00:28:54,170 --> 00:28:55,010
basically.

612
00:28:55,010 --> 00:28:59,000
The first two have very
semantic level representations.

613
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:02,090
UL2 is a large scale
encoder/decoder model.

614
00:29:02,090 --> 00:29:04,620
MetaCLIP is our
internal CLIP model.

615
00:29:04,620 --> 00:29:09,350
And the third one has more
of a character level text

616
00:29:09,350 --> 00:29:11,070
representation by T5.

617
00:29:11,070 --> 00:29:16,320
So we encode the text prompt
using all three of these,

618
00:29:16,320 --> 00:29:19,440
we project them all to the model
dimension, and we concatenate.

619
00:29:19,440 --> 00:29:22,613
And that gives us
our text sequence.

620
00:29:22,613 --> 00:29:25,665

621
00:29:25,665 --> 00:29:27,290
The second thing we
need to do-- you'll

622
00:29:27,290 --> 00:29:28,790
remember from the
learning objective

623
00:29:28,790 --> 00:29:33,830
that we also need to
condition on the time step.

624
00:29:33,830 --> 00:29:36,530
So what do we do here is, we
do this in adaptive layer norm

625
00:29:36,530 --> 00:29:37,290
blocks.

626
00:29:37,290 --> 00:29:39,390
So we've already added
a cross-attention block.

627
00:29:39,390 --> 00:29:42,290
We also add this adaptive
layer norm block.

628
00:29:42,290 --> 00:29:45,260
This might seem like
quite a strange way

629
00:29:45,260 --> 00:29:46,740
of adding some conditioning.

630
00:29:46,740 --> 00:29:49,220
It's something that was
popularized in the Diffusion

631
00:29:49,220 --> 00:29:53,390
Transformer paper, which was the
first paper that used diffusion

632
00:29:53,390 --> 00:29:57,860
with transformers--
obviously, by the name.

633
00:29:57,860 --> 00:29:59,700
It might seem a
little bit random.

634
00:29:59,700 --> 00:30:01,420
It's basically very
computationally cheap

635
00:30:01,420 --> 00:30:02,745
and works super well.

636
00:30:02,745 --> 00:30:05,750

637
00:30:05,750 --> 00:30:08,890
Number 3-- there
are only three--

638
00:30:08,890 --> 00:30:12,070
we use full
bidirectional attention.

639
00:30:12,070 --> 00:30:14,950
Llama is an
autoregressive text model

640
00:30:14,950 --> 00:30:18,490
that use causal masking
for next token prediction.

641
00:30:18,490 --> 00:30:21,620
For the flow matching objective,
we have no such constraints.

642
00:30:21,620 --> 00:30:25,090
We want every video token to
see every other video token.

643
00:30:25,090 --> 00:30:29,500
We don't care about causal
masking, so we take that out.

644
00:30:29,500 --> 00:30:31,690
A very low level detail is
that because of this, we

645
00:30:31,690 --> 00:30:34,490
use multi-head attention instead
of grouped query attention.

646
00:30:34,490 --> 00:30:35,510
But that's everything.

647
00:30:35,510 --> 00:30:39,520
Other than that, it's
the Llama architecture.

648
00:30:39,520 --> 00:30:44,900
So we now have our full
architecture diagram.

649
00:30:44,900 --> 00:30:47,080
I was very wary of putting
this earlier because it's

650
00:30:47,080 --> 00:30:49,480
pretty complicated, but
I think it should all

651
00:30:49,480 --> 00:30:52,360
make sense at this point.

652
00:30:52,360 --> 00:30:55,412
So from left to right,
during training,

653
00:30:55,412 --> 00:30:56,870
we take one of our
training videos.

654
00:30:56,870 --> 00:31:00,290
We encode it with the TAE to
get to our compressed latent

655
00:31:00,290 --> 00:31:01,470
representation.

656
00:31:01,470 --> 00:31:02,880
It goes through
a small patchify.

657
00:31:02,880 --> 00:31:05,270
This is just does some
extra compression.

658
00:31:05,270 --> 00:31:10,400
And we flatten it, and that's
how we get our input sequence.

659
00:31:10,400 --> 00:31:13,400
During training, we'll
construct our training, sample

660
00:31:13,400 --> 00:31:16,230
by combining it with
this Gaussian noise.

661
00:31:16,230 --> 00:31:18,380
During inference,
this whole sequence

662
00:31:18,380 --> 00:31:20,630
will start off being
Gaussian noise.

663
00:31:20,630 --> 00:31:24,570
The sequence goes through
these Llama transformer blocks.

664
00:31:24,570 --> 00:31:30,290
We add in the conditioning, and
then we get our output sequence.

665
00:31:30,290 --> 00:31:33,330
During training, we compute
our loss and backpropagate.

666
00:31:33,330 --> 00:31:36,500
During inference, we would do
this iterative denoising process

667
00:31:36,500 --> 00:31:41,870
and finally decode
back to the RGB space.

668
00:31:41,870 --> 00:31:47,240
Any questions on
the architecture?

669
00:31:47,240 --> 00:31:48,180
Question about inputs.

670
00:31:48,180 --> 00:31:50,150
So you start off
with Gaussian noise

671
00:31:50,150 --> 00:31:52,670
and then you have a sequence
of denoising steps [INAUDIBLE].

672
00:31:52,670 --> 00:31:55,280
But where does the
text prompt go?

673
00:31:55,280 --> 00:31:58,560
Why doesn't it generate a
random video [INAUDIBLE]?

674
00:31:58,560 --> 00:32:06,010
So at inference, for
a given text prompt,

675
00:32:06,010 --> 00:32:08,860
we add this conditioning
into the model always.

676
00:32:08,860 --> 00:32:11,140
So the input sequence
is just Gaussian noise.

677
00:32:11,140 --> 00:32:13,240
But that's not what the
text information comes in.

678
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:14,790
The text information
comes in through

679
00:32:14,790 --> 00:32:16,360
these cross-attention layers.

680
00:32:16,360 --> 00:32:18,880
So even though the input
sequence is just noise,

681
00:32:18,880 --> 00:32:22,510
the model is still seeing
these clean text information.

682
00:32:22,510 --> 00:32:25,260
We don't noise the text
or anything like that.

683
00:32:25,260 --> 00:32:28,350
Does that make--
does that make sense?

684
00:32:28,350 --> 00:32:28,990
Two questions.

685
00:32:28,990 --> 00:32:34,110
One, I noticed this segment
[INAUDIBLE] in the equations,

686
00:32:34,110 --> 00:32:37,920
that you don't see noise
all the way to clean data.

687
00:32:37,920 --> 00:32:40,530
Does that mean-- is that
segmentation making sure

688
00:32:40,530 --> 00:32:45,660
that you always get diversity in
the same query every single time

689
00:32:45,660 --> 00:32:48,390
you go through the
denoising process?

690
00:32:48,390 --> 00:32:52,011
You actually go all
the way to pdata.

691
00:32:52,011 --> 00:32:55,313
It's just a hyperparameter
that we keep constant.

692
00:32:55,313 --> 00:32:58,023

693
00:32:58,023 --> 00:33:00,440
I cannot remember off the top
of my head exactly what that

694
00:33:00,440 --> 00:33:02,630
hyperparameter is for, actually.

695
00:33:02,630 --> 00:33:04,970
I'm going to talk to
you about that after.

696
00:33:04,970 --> 00:33:08,570
The other question is, how many
denoising steps is typically

697
00:33:08,570 --> 00:33:09,410
required?

698
00:33:09,410 --> 00:33:13,470
So it's a very good point.

699
00:33:13,470 --> 00:33:16,650
So during inference, you'll
sample a series of time steps.

700
00:33:16,650 --> 00:33:20,010
Usually, with these flow-based
models, the more you sample,

701
00:33:20,010 --> 00:33:22,310
the better you'll better
approximate these probability

702
00:33:22,310 --> 00:33:23,480
paths.

703
00:33:23,480 --> 00:33:25,440
In practice, we use 250--

704
00:33:25,440 --> 00:33:26,990
I think.

705
00:33:26,990 --> 00:33:28,850
One of the advantages
of flow matching

706
00:33:28,850 --> 00:33:31,770
is that the probability paths
are theoretically straighter.

707
00:33:31,770 --> 00:33:36,170
So you should be able to require
less function evaluations

708
00:33:36,170 --> 00:33:38,000
to approximate this path.

709
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:39,260
That's one of the advantages.

710
00:33:39,260 --> 00:33:41,000
Is that 250 like--

711
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:49,040
so there's not a pre-described
number of TAEs during training?

712
00:33:49,040 --> 00:33:51,110
Yeah, that's continuous.

713
00:33:51,110 --> 00:33:54,680
But during inference, we just
sample some discrete ones.

714
00:33:54,680 --> 00:33:57,370

715
00:33:57,370 --> 00:33:59,760
So I have a general
question on the two videos

716
00:33:59,760 --> 00:34:03,570
that you showed the difference
between 2022 and 2024.

717
00:34:03,570 --> 00:34:06,010
Was the improvement
just algorithmic,

718
00:34:06,010 --> 00:34:09,360
or did you also improve the hard
work in terms of better GPUs,

719
00:34:09,360 --> 00:34:12,750
or it was just a pure
algorithm improvement?

720
00:34:12,750 --> 00:34:18,929
So I think everything
comes down to scale.

721
00:34:18,929 --> 00:34:21,270
There are quite a lot of
things that have changed.

722
00:34:21,270 --> 00:34:23,980
There's an architecture change
moving towards transformers,

723
00:34:23,980 --> 00:34:26,460
and then there's the
scale, and that includes

724
00:34:26,460 --> 00:34:27,420
a whole load of things.

725
00:34:27,420 --> 00:34:32,040
Scaling the data scaling
the amount of compute

726
00:34:32,040 --> 00:34:34,346
in order for that to
be like tractable.

727
00:34:34,346 --> 00:34:37,170

728
00:34:37,170 --> 00:34:39,471
Better GPU hardware does help.

729
00:34:39,471 --> 00:34:41,429
There wasn't a huge
improvement in GPU hardware

730
00:34:41,429 --> 00:34:43,560
over those two years.

731
00:34:43,560 --> 00:34:46,820
It might have been one or two
generations of NVIDIA's stuff.

732
00:34:46,820 --> 00:34:55,900

733
00:34:55,900 --> 00:34:57,650
So now, we have an
architecture that we're

734
00:34:57,650 --> 00:35:00,920
pretty confident scales.

735
00:35:00,920 --> 00:35:03,410
We have a learning
objective that we think

736
00:35:03,410 --> 00:35:05,130
should work in flow matching.

737
00:35:05,130 --> 00:35:07,560
That's pretty much
it for the details.

738
00:35:07,560 --> 00:35:10,795
It's not the entire story,
but that's a lot of it.

739
00:35:10,795 --> 00:35:13,880

740
00:35:13,880 --> 00:35:19,340
So the last technical details
are about data and the training

741
00:35:19,340 --> 00:35:21,350
recipe.

742
00:35:21,350 --> 00:35:24,590
So data.

743
00:35:24,590 --> 00:35:27,530
I think in a lot of ways, this
is the most important slide

744
00:35:27,530 --> 00:35:30,260
of the entire talk today.

745
00:35:30,260 --> 00:35:33,618
Data is so important for
training large language models.

746
00:35:33,618 --> 00:35:35,660
And by the way, when I
say large language models,

747
00:35:35,660 --> 00:35:37,190
I'm just talking
about transformers

748
00:35:37,190 --> 00:35:39,030
at scale for any modality.

749
00:35:39,030 --> 00:35:41,050
People use different
definitions there.

750
00:35:41,050 --> 00:35:43,700

751
00:35:43,700 --> 00:35:47,400
But these models are
incredibly data hungry.

752
00:35:47,400 --> 00:35:49,500
They require
internet scale data,

753
00:35:49,500 --> 00:35:53,040
and they require the
data to be clean.

754
00:35:53,040 --> 00:35:55,510
The scaling laws depend
on the data being cleaned.

755
00:35:55,510 --> 00:35:58,110
Otherwise, the scaling
laws don't hold.

756
00:35:58,110 --> 00:36:00,150
And the model output
quality depends

757
00:36:00,150 --> 00:36:01,530
on the data being cleaned.

758
00:36:01,530 --> 00:36:06,690
As a result of this, research
groups at these big companies

759
00:36:06,690 --> 00:36:09,028
spend a huge amount
of resources on data.

760
00:36:09,028 --> 00:36:11,070
This is something that I
find isn't really talked

761
00:36:11,070 --> 00:36:12,190
about so much.

762
00:36:12,190 --> 00:36:15,000
But they'll spend huge amounts
of resources in terms of GPUs

763
00:36:15,000 --> 00:36:17,320
and also actual researchers.

764
00:36:17,320 --> 00:36:21,360
Often, on these research
teams, the data teams massively

765
00:36:21,360 --> 00:36:24,990
outnumber the
modeling teams, which

766
00:36:24,990 --> 00:36:30,600
was something very new to
me after my little PhD.

767
00:36:30,600 --> 00:36:31,720
So why is this?

768
00:36:31,720 --> 00:36:36,420
Well, remember we're
training generative models.

769
00:36:36,420 --> 00:36:39,510
We are learning this
distribution of our training

770
00:36:39,510 --> 00:36:41,970
data, and then we're
sampling videos from it

771
00:36:41,970 --> 00:36:44,350
that are likely according
to that training data.

772
00:36:44,350 --> 00:36:47,190
So if we want to sample
the kinds of videos

773
00:36:47,190 --> 00:36:49,160
that I showed you in
the first two slides,

774
00:36:49,160 --> 00:36:51,675
then all of our training
data needs to look like that.

775
00:36:51,675 --> 00:36:54,790

776
00:36:54,790 --> 00:36:56,588
I'm mainly talking
about pre-training here.

777
00:36:56,588 --> 00:36:58,630
You obviously have a
post-training phase as well,

778
00:36:58,630 --> 00:37:03,140
where you can align your videos
to be more high quality perhaps.

779
00:37:03,140 --> 00:37:07,120
But you're pre-training data
still needs to look great.

780
00:37:07,120 --> 00:37:10,720
So this is a huge challenge.

781
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:15,250
We train the model on the order
of around 100 million videos.

782
00:37:15,250 --> 00:37:16,640
How do we get to this number?

783
00:37:16,640 --> 00:37:19,690
Well, we can predict
a training budget

784
00:37:19,690 --> 00:37:21,080
that we had for this project.

785
00:37:21,080 --> 00:37:23,620
And you want as many
videos as you can.

786
00:37:23,620 --> 00:37:26,542
You don't epoch very much.

787
00:37:26,542 --> 00:37:28,750
So the challenge was how to
get this many videos that

788
00:37:28,750 --> 00:37:31,480
are high enough quality.

789
00:37:31,480 --> 00:37:33,790
At the time of Movie
Gen, we constructed

790
00:37:33,790 --> 00:37:37,600
this incredibly detailed,
complex pipeline

791
00:37:37,600 --> 00:37:41,045
with a bunch of handcrafted
and model-based filters.

792
00:37:41,045 --> 00:37:42,670
I'll just talk through
a few bits of it

793
00:37:42,670 --> 00:37:47,190
because of the sheer amount
of work that went into this.

794
00:37:47,190 --> 00:37:54,630
So you start with a large pool
of videos from some corpus.

795
00:37:54,630 --> 00:37:57,660
They may be different lengths,
long tail of concepts.

796
00:37:57,660 --> 00:38:00,390
We did a bunch of visual
filtering on these--

797
00:38:00,390 --> 00:38:03,190
removing videos that are
too small, scene changes,

798
00:38:03,190 --> 00:38:04,830
bad aesthetics.

799
00:38:04,830 --> 00:38:08,460
We removed a bunch of
videos that have bad motion.

800
00:38:08,460 --> 00:38:11,700
It turns out a bunch of
videos in any large corpus

801
00:38:11,700 --> 00:38:16,030
have really slow motion,
janky motion, motion effects.

802
00:38:16,030 --> 00:38:17,730
We removed all of those.

803
00:38:17,730 --> 00:38:23,770
We then did a content
filtering step.

804
00:38:23,770 --> 00:38:26,790
This is first deduplication, but
the really important one here

805
00:38:26,790 --> 00:38:28,590
is resampling.

806
00:38:28,590 --> 00:38:31,230
Large language models
do not work well

807
00:38:31,230 --> 00:38:34,200
when they're trained on a
very imbalanced data set

808
00:38:34,200 --> 00:38:35,320
in terms of concepts.

809
00:38:35,320 --> 00:38:36,910
So something with
a very long tail.

810
00:38:36,910 --> 00:38:41,010
They work best if the concept
distribution is roughly, roughly

811
00:38:41,010 --> 00:38:43,170
uniform.

812
00:38:43,170 --> 00:38:45,610
And that uniformity
doesn't occur if you just

813
00:38:45,610 --> 00:38:47,600
take a random set of videos.

814
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:52,390
So we do this very complex
visual concept extraction,

815
00:38:52,390 --> 00:38:53,210
clustering.

816
00:38:53,210 --> 00:38:58,660
We upweight certain clusters,
downweight certain clusters.

817
00:38:58,660 --> 00:39:01,480
All of this will give
us a set of videos.

818
00:39:01,480 --> 00:39:03,040
We also need captions
because we're

819
00:39:03,040 --> 00:39:04,580
doing text-to-video generation.

820
00:39:04,580 --> 00:39:10,000
And we generate these
automatically using Llama 3.

821
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:12,940
So that is data.

822
00:39:12,940 --> 00:39:16,540
Very lastly, the
training recipe.

823
00:39:16,540 --> 00:39:19,840
So this multi-stage
recipe here was optimized

824
00:39:19,840 --> 00:39:22,030
for convergence speed.

825
00:39:22,030 --> 00:39:26,968
We start off with a 256 pixel
T2I stage image generation.

826
00:39:26,968 --> 00:39:29,260
Here, are the model can whip
through a bunch of samples

827
00:39:29,260 --> 00:39:34,090
in very few
relatively GPU hours.

828
00:39:34,090 --> 00:39:37,750
We then move on to a
pre-training stage with joint

829
00:39:37,750 --> 00:39:39,800
text-to-image and
text-to-video generation,

830
00:39:39,800 --> 00:39:43,860
where we progressively increase
the resolution from two 256p

831
00:39:43,860 --> 00:39:46,050
to 768p.

832
00:39:46,050 --> 00:39:49,420
At the highest
resolution here, 768p,

833
00:39:49,420 --> 00:39:52,500
that's where we have the
sequence length of 73K.

834
00:39:52,500 --> 00:39:58,830
And we trained this on 6,000
GPUs around 1,500 batch size.

835
00:39:58,830 --> 00:40:01,360
At this point, the
model splinters.

836
00:40:01,360 --> 00:40:04,390
So we have a text-to-video
post-training stage.

837
00:40:04,390 --> 00:40:09,430
This is just SFT on a very small
set of very high-quality videos.

838
00:40:09,430 --> 00:40:12,370
And then we also branch off into
these different capabilities.

839
00:40:12,370 --> 00:40:14,220
I'm not going to talk
about those a bunch,

840
00:40:14,220 --> 00:40:17,790
but I'll show you
some examples later.

841
00:40:17,790 --> 00:40:19,800
Any questions?

842
00:40:19,800 --> 00:40:21,300
Sure.

843
00:40:21,300 --> 00:40:24,936
A wider tailed
data set of videos.

844
00:40:24,936 --> 00:40:28,410
Is there pre-training
you can do specifically

845
00:40:28,410 --> 00:40:30,570
to address those
kinds of challenges?

846
00:40:30,570 --> 00:40:31,120
Yeah.

847
00:40:31,120 --> 00:40:36,480
I think-- definitely.

848
00:40:36,480 --> 00:40:39,790
I guess the question is
about certain concepts

849
00:40:39,790 --> 00:40:41,030
in your pre-training data.

850
00:40:41,030 --> 00:40:44,360
I guess, in a very large
corpus we'll be training on,

851
00:40:44,360 --> 00:40:46,850
there's an incredibly
long tail of concepts.

852
00:40:46,850 --> 00:40:50,380
Pretty much every concept that
you might want to generate

853
00:40:50,380 --> 00:40:52,920
would probably appear at
some point in that data set.

854
00:40:52,920 --> 00:40:56,200

855
00:40:56,200 --> 00:40:57,105
Anything else?

856
00:40:57,105 --> 00:40:59,890

857
00:40:59,890 --> 00:41:04,238
So lastly, the results
and applications.

858
00:41:04,238 --> 00:41:06,530
So putting everything together
that I just showed you--

859
00:41:06,530 --> 00:41:08,738
and that's all of the
technical details, by the way--

860
00:41:08,738 --> 00:41:10,930
we published all of this--

861
00:41:10,930 --> 00:41:14,410
we get videos like
this and the ones

862
00:41:14,410 --> 00:41:18,700
that I showed at the
beginning of the talk as well.

863
00:41:18,700 --> 00:41:22,610
And this pretty much sums up
the whole point of this project.

864
00:41:22,610 --> 00:41:25,870
I really want to hammer home
that these kinds of videos

865
00:41:25,870 --> 00:41:27,940
were nowhere near
possible before people

866
00:41:27,940 --> 00:41:31,210
started scaling transformers.

867
00:41:31,210 --> 00:41:34,780
And we showed here that, again,
this classic architecture

868
00:41:34,780 --> 00:41:38,160
unification story, scaling data
model parameters and compute

869
00:41:38,160 --> 00:41:40,530
for a simple
transformer, ended up

870
00:41:40,530 --> 00:41:45,030
in a model that can reason about
objects, motion, and physics

871
00:41:45,030 --> 00:41:48,000
just by watching videos.

872
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:51,120
So none of this was possible,
and it's scaling transformers

873
00:41:51,120 --> 00:41:52,445
that unlocks all of this.

874
00:41:52,445 --> 00:41:54,845

875
00:41:54,845 --> 00:41:56,220
I have to keep on
pressing replay

876
00:41:56,220 --> 00:41:58,345
because I couldn't figure
out how to do auto-replay

877
00:41:58,345 --> 00:42:00,030
on Google Slides.

878
00:42:00,030 --> 00:42:05,380
How many PhDs does it
take to auto-replay?

879
00:42:05,380 --> 00:42:06,570
So another one.

880
00:42:06,570 --> 00:42:10,800
Here's a sloth with pink
sunglasses on a donut float.

881
00:42:10,800 --> 00:42:16,890
Again, I think the physics of
sloth-like donut flirtation

882
00:42:16,890 --> 00:42:19,140
here are good.

883
00:42:19,140 --> 00:42:22,110
This slide isn't only a joke.

884
00:42:22,110 --> 00:42:24,660
It does actually highlight
something quite important.

885
00:42:24,660 --> 00:42:27,010
When we're training
generative models,

886
00:42:27,010 --> 00:42:29,190
one important thing
is how well it

887
00:42:29,190 --> 00:42:31,140
generalizes to concepts
that maybe weren't

888
00:42:31,140 --> 00:42:32,860
in its pre-training data.

889
00:42:32,860 --> 00:42:37,330
We can't be sure that there
are no sloths in floats

890
00:42:37,330 --> 00:42:38,480
in the pre-training data.

891
00:42:38,480 --> 00:42:41,300
But by common sense, there
probably aren't too many.

892
00:42:41,300 --> 00:42:43,060
So the fact that it
can generate this

893
00:42:43,060 --> 00:42:46,586
is testament to its
generalizing capabilities.

894
00:42:46,586 --> 00:42:52,240

895
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:53,150
A few other things.

896
00:42:53,150 --> 00:42:55,420
I showed you some of
these at the beginning.

897
00:42:55,420 --> 00:42:58,950
We also trained a
Movie Gen edit model.

898
00:42:58,950 --> 00:43:01,750
Like I said before, you take the
original video on the top left

899
00:43:01,750 --> 00:43:05,480
and you can provide these
precise edit instructions.

900
00:43:05,480 --> 00:43:09,010
This is really magic to me.

901
00:43:09,010 --> 00:43:11,300
I didn't get a chance to
cover this too much today,

902
00:43:11,300 --> 00:43:17,140
but the team trained
this with paired data.

903
00:43:17,140 --> 00:43:18,940
It's incredibly hard
to get paired data

904
00:43:18,940 --> 00:43:21,010
for this task-- these
pairs of input and outputs.

905
00:43:21,010 --> 00:43:23,910
They came up with a fascinating
self-supervised approach

906
00:43:23,910 --> 00:43:24,410
for this.

907
00:43:24,410 --> 00:43:27,520
I'd really recommend
reading the paper.

908
00:43:27,520 --> 00:43:30,970
Where did you guys get the
data from on this project?

909
00:43:30,970 --> 00:43:35,005
The videos are completely
licensed videos by Masha.

910
00:43:35,005 --> 00:43:47,360

911
00:43:47,360 --> 00:43:49,280
And one more example here.

912
00:43:49,280 --> 00:43:52,340
You can take-- everyone wants
to take a video of their penguin

913
00:43:52,340 --> 00:43:55,730
and put some Victorian
outfits on it.

914
00:43:55,730 --> 00:43:56,905
So this is great.

915
00:43:56,905 --> 00:44:01,550

916
00:44:01,550 --> 00:44:06,510
One other model that we trained
is the personalization model.

917
00:44:06,510 --> 00:44:09,770
So here, this is
Movie Gen video.

918
00:44:09,770 --> 00:44:11,780
But with the added
capability, you

919
00:44:11,780 --> 00:44:14,040
can condition on an
image of yourself.

920
00:44:14,040 --> 00:44:17,390
So here, the model can
generate a video that

921
00:44:17,390 --> 00:44:20,750
is faithful to the text prompt,
but also contains the person

922
00:44:20,750 --> 00:44:21,965
in the conditioning image.

923
00:44:21,965 --> 00:44:22,840
So that's really fun.

924
00:44:22,840 --> 00:44:25,550

925
00:44:25,550 --> 00:44:27,900
And another funny example here.

926
00:44:27,900 --> 00:44:30,590
This is my colleague that I
worked on the project with.

927
00:44:30,590 --> 00:44:33,260
This makes me laugh.

928
00:44:33,260 --> 00:44:35,820
So if anyone is interested in
this, please go read the paper.

929
00:44:35,820 --> 00:44:39,050
A bunch of work went into this.

930
00:44:39,050 --> 00:44:42,235
Very lastly, we're going to see
now if the theater has audio.

931
00:44:42,235 --> 00:44:44,990

932
00:44:44,990 --> 00:44:46,910
It doesn't.

933
00:44:46,910 --> 00:44:50,210
So we trained this
Movie Gen audio model.

934
00:44:50,210 --> 00:44:52,380
Again, I didn't have time
to go into this today.

935
00:44:52,380 --> 00:44:56,180
But this is a model that will
condition on text and a video,

936
00:44:56,180 --> 00:44:59,310
either real or generated, and
generate synchronized audio.

937
00:44:59,310 --> 00:45:03,440
So this way, we can add audio
to our generated videos.

938
00:45:03,440 --> 00:45:05,430
It's an amazing research
team that did this.

939
00:45:05,430 --> 00:45:07,305
So I'd recommend going
to check out the paper

940
00:45:07,305 --> 00:45:10,290
if you're interested.

941
00:45:10,290 --> 00:45:13,470
So there are a couple
of just points left.

942
00:45:13,470 --> 00:45:15,860
Firstly, some
quantitative results.

943
00:45:15,860 --> 00:45:21,600
It's incredibly hard to do fair
comparisons in video generation.

944
00:45:21,600 --> 00:45:25,910
We don't have automated
metrics or anything like this.

945
00:45:25,910 --> 00:45:30,020
So what we did was, we did a
very extensive human evaluation

946
00:45:30,020 --> 00:45:31,010
study.

947
00:45:31,010 --> 00:45:32,900
We came up with a
bunch of metrics

948
00:45:32,900 --> 00:45:34,940
that are somewhat orthogonal
to each other that

949
00:45:34,940 --> 00:45:38,570
test every aspect of video
generation, things like motion

950
00:45:38,570 --> 00:45:42,300
quality, how well the videos
follow, the text prompts,

951
00:45:42,300 --> 00:45:45,110
visual quality.

952
00:45:45,110 --> 00:45:46,770
These metrics are
shown on the left.

953
00:45:46,770 --> 00:45:48,720
I won't go into a huge
bunch of detail here,

954
00:45:48,720 --> 00:45:52,050
but they're very fully
defined in the paper.

955
00:45:52,050 --> 00:45:55,850
We put in a load of work to make
sure that the human evaluators

956
00:45:55,850 --> 00:46:00,560
had low standard deviation when
multiple evaluators were rating

957
00:46:00,560 --> 00:46:01,550
the same thing.

958
00:46:01,550 --> 00:46:05,300
So we compared to all
of the methods that

959
00:46:05,300 --> 00:46:06,840
were released in the same year.

960
00:46:06,840 --> 00:46:08,210
This is 2024.

961
00:46:08,210 --> 00:46:13,820
So things like runways models,
Luma, Sora at the time,

962
00:46:13,820 --> 00:46:15,320
and KLING.

963
00:46:15,320 --> 00:46:18,900
And these are net win
rates you're seeing here.

964
00:46:18,900 --> 00:46:23,270
So a score above zero means
that our model was preferred.

965
00:46:23,270 --> 00:46:25,220
At the time of
release, Movie Gen

966
00:46:25,220 --> 00:46:26,760
outperformed all
of the prior work.

967
00:46:26,760 --> 00:46:28,155
So that's great.

968
00:46:28,155 --> 00:46:30,940

969
00:46:30,940 --> 00:46:34,180
It's very hard to
draw conclusions here.

970
00:46:34,180 --> 00:46:35,890
The one conclusion
we can draw is

971
00:46:35,890 --> 00:46:41,470
that Movie Gen was better than
these at the time of release.

972
00:46:41,470 --> 00:46:43,840
Like researchers,
what we like to do

973
00:46:43,840 --> 00:46:46,150
at this point is
to look into all

974
00:46:46,150 --> 00:46:47,950
of the technical reports
of the prior work

975
00:46:47,950 --> 00:46:49,570
and see what they
did differently,

976
00:46:49,570 --> 00:46:53,740
and what did Movie
Gen do differently?

977
00:46:53,740 --> 00:46:56,750
Because we'd like to conclude--
what led to these improvements?

978
00:46:56,750 --> 00:46:57,890
Was it more compute?

979
00:46:57,890 --> 00:46:59,240
Was it flow matching?

980
00:46:59,240 --> 00:47:01,780
Was it better data filtration?

981
00:47:01,780 --> 00:47:03,820
Things like that.

982
00:47:03,820 --> 00:47:07,300
But it's, unfortunately,
not possible in today's age

983
00:47:07,300 --> 00:47:10,670
to do that because we don't
see research publications.

984
00:47:10,670 --> 00:47:14,200
But what we do know is that
all of the technical details

985
00:47:14,200 --> 00:47:16,840
I just presented work, and
they work really, really well.

986
00:47:16,840 --> 00:47:19,330
And they're a good
starting point for anyone

987
00:47:19,330 --> 00:47:23,320
who's looking to improve
text-to-video generation.

988
00:47:23,320 --> 00:47:26,760
And we hope the community does.

989
00:47:26,760 --> 00:47:31,070
The very last technical
thing, scaling laws.

990
00:47:31,070 --> 00:47:32,840
I've talked throughout
the whole talk

991
00:47:32,840 --> 00:47:37,940
today about architecture
unification across modalities

992
00:47:37,940 --> 00:47:39,740
and learning
objectives, and this

993
00:47:39,740 --> 00:47:43,770
was a really nice result that we
found at the end of the project.

994
00:47:43,770 --> 00:47:48,860
So what you're seeing here
is a scaling law graph.

995
00:47:48,860 --> 00:47:51,770
How we use these when we're
training large language models

996
00:47:51,770 --> 00:47:59,030
is often when you start
training like a GPT, or a Llama,

997
00:47:59,030 --> 00:48:01,920
or whatever, you have a
certain training budget.

998
00:48:01,920 --> 00:48:04,490
This is how many months
you have and how many

999
00:48:04,490 --> 00:48:08,090
GPUs you have for that period.

1000
00:48:08,090 --> 00:48:11,070
But if you know that
training compute budget,

1001
00:48:11,070 --> 00:48:13,467
an open question is how
big your model should be.

1002
00:48:13,467 --> 00:48:16,050
What should be the optimal model
size for that compute budget?

1003
00:48:16,050 --> 00:48:17,342
It could be a very small model.

1004
00:48:17,342 --> 00:48:19,084
You train for more--

1005
00:48:19,084 --> 00:48:22,890
you train for-- more
iterations or a larger model,

1006
00:48:22,890 --> 00:48:24,210
you train for less.

1007
00:48:24,210 --> 00:48:27,990
These scaling law curves are
for estimating the optimal model

1008
00:48:27,990 --> 00:48:30,120
size for a given compute budget.

1009
00:48:30,120 --> 00:48:32,830
So just looking at
the blue crosses,

1010
00:48:32,830 --> 00:48:34,980
we plotted a few of
these data points

1011
00:48:34,980 --> 00:48:38,280
for a Movie Gen. Remember,
Movie Gen, text-to-video model,

1012
00:48:38,280 --> 00:48:40,140
Llama 3 architecture.

1013
00:48:40,140 --> 00:48:42,000
And looking at the
blue crosses, we

1014
00:48:42,000 --> 00:48:43,590
can see this nice
correlation we often

1015
00:48:43,590 --> 00:48:45,790
see in scaling laws
with transformers.

1016
00:48:45,790 --> 00:48:49,210
We then overlaid the
Llama 3 scaling law.

1017
00:48:49,210 --> 00:48:53,130
So this is the scaling
law for the text model.

1018
00:48:53,130 --> 00:48:56,220
And amazingly, we see
that the Llama 3 scaling

1019
00:48:56,220 --> 00:48:58,170
law for this
text-only model serves

1020
00:48:58,170 --> 00:49:01,200
as a reasonable
predictor for model size

1021
00:49:01,200 --> 00:49:04,360
and compute for
video generation.

1022
00:49:04,360 --> 00:49:08,070
And this seems to hint that
scaling laws for transformers

1023
00:49:08,070 --> 00:49:12,526
are maybe modality independent,
which is pretty fascinating.

1024
00:49:12,526 --> 00:49:16,570

1025
00:49:16,570 --> 00:49:19,060
So we're on the last part now.

1026
00:49:19,060 --> 00:49:19,655
What is next?

1027
00:49:19,655 --> 00:49:23,000

1028
00:49:23,000 --> 00:49:26,370
Movie Gen did not
solve video generation.

1029
00:49:26,370 --> 00:49:29,180

1030
00:49:29,180 --> 00:49:31,740
There are still
lots of problems.

1031
00:49:31,740 --> 00:49:35,000
The model will struggle with
generating complex motions

1032
00:49:35,000 --> 00:49:37,640
from complex prompts.

1033
00:49:37,640 --> 00:49:39,410
An example here is
a dramatic scene

1034
00:49:39,410 --> 00:49:43,490
of two cars colliding
at an intersection.

1035
00:49:43,490 --> 00:49:46,110
So it's looking pretty
good at this point.

1036
00:49:46,110 --> 00:49:49,275
And then at some point
near the end, they--

1037
00:49:49,275 --> 00:49:51,380
I don't what you'd call that--

1038
00:49:51,380 --> 00:49:55,760
they independently implode.

1039
00:49:55,760 --> 00:49:59,880
At one point, the silver car
turns into two cars as well.

1040
00:49:59,880 --> 00:50:02,810
So this is a random
generation from our model.

1041
00:50:02,810 --> 00:50:06,710
So text-to-video
generation is not solved.

1042
00:50:06,710 --> 00:50:15,680
So my final slide is some
ideas about where I think

1043
00:50:15,680 --> 00:50:17,850
video generation is going next.

1044
00:50:17,850 --> 00:50:21,000
Things that I'm sure we'll
see some version of at some

1045
00:50:21,000 --> 00:50:21,820
point soon.

1046
00:50:21,820 --> 00:50:24,220
So what is next?

1047
00:50:24,220 --> 00:50:29,230
How can we solve the issues that
you saw in the previous slides?

1048
00:50:29,230 --> 00:50:36,070
Well, the first obvious one
is scaling everything more.

1049
00:50:36,070 --> 00:50:38,250
This has been like the
story of machine learning

1050
00:50:38,250 --> 00:50:42,630
for the last six
years or something.

1051
00:50:42,630 --> 00:50:44,920
And I think this
would definitely work.

1052
00:50:44,920 --> 00:50:47,440
Movie Gen was a $30
billion parameter model.

1053
00:50:47,440 --> 00:50:48,670
It was based on Llama 3.

1054
00:50:48,670 --> 00:50:51,030
The largest Llama
3 model was 405B.

1055
00:50:51,030 --> 00:50:54,300
I think scaling everything
more would definitely

1056
00:50:54,300 --> 00:50:58,530
result in far higher
quality generations.

1057
00:50:58,530 --> 00:51:02,670
Some challenges there would
be around scaling data

1058
00:51:02,670 --> 00:51:04,296
by an order of magnitude.

1059
00:51:04,296 --> 00:51:07,590

1060
00:51:07,590 --> 00:51:10,530
Number 2 is reasoning.

1061
00:51:10,530 --> 00:51:14,640
We've all seen the amazing
benefits in language modeling

1062
00:51:14,640 --> 00:51:20,870
that have come from reasoning
in the last year or two.

1063
00:51:20,870 --> 00:51:25,890
Here, the reasoning gives the
model the ability to pause,

1064
00:51:25,890 --> 00:51:28,020
think, generate a
chain of thoughts,

1065
00:51:28,020 --> 00:51:34,100
self-correct before
generating an answer.

1066
00:51:34,100 --> 00:51:37,040
I think it's very clear that
video generation models would

1067
00:51:37,040 --> 00:51:40,020
benefit from this kind of
reasoning capability as well.

1068
00:51:40,020 --> 00:51:42,350
I think that could
result in a step change.

1069
00:51:42,350 --> 00:51:44,120
When all of us were
looking at the videos

1070
00:51:44,120 --> 00:51:46,190
on the previous slide,
it was very clear

1071
00:51:46,190 --> 00:51:47,700
that something was wrong.

1072
00:51:47,700 --> 00:51:51,140
It looked very obvious to us.

1073
00:51:51,140 --> 00:51:56,300
It doesn't seem too
unfathomable to be

1074
00:51:56,300 --> 00:51:59,180
able to imbue some
video generation

1075
00:51:59,180 --> 00:52:02,370
model with the capability
to self-correct,

1076
00:52:02,370 --> 00:52:04,850
maybe see that there is
some errors in the video

1077
00:52:04,850 --> 00:52:06,700
that it generated
and correct it.

1078
00:52:06,700 --> 00:52:09,470

1079
00:52:09,470 --> 00:52:11,920
There are lots of really
interesting research questions

1080
00:52:11,920 --> 00:52:12,420
here.

1081
00:52:12,420 --> 00:52:15,110
Like what does it mean to
generate a reasoning trace

1082
00:52:15,110 --> 00:52:16,140
for media generation?

1083
00:52:16,140 --> 00:52:18,140
You might have seen these
chain of thoughts that

1084
00:52:18,140 --> 00:52:22,780
are generated by like R1 or R3.

1085
00:52:22,780 --> 00:52:26,020
I wonder what that looks
like for media generation.

1086
00:52:26,020 --> 00:52:28,430
The other issue
is how to verify.

1087
00:52:28,430 --> 00:52:34,300
So these latest state of
the art reasoning approaches

1088
00:52:34,300 --> 00:52:35,530
are all trained with RL.

1089
00:52:35,530 --> 00:52:38,950
RL requires verification
models to verify

1090
00:52:38,950 --> 00:52:40,930
the correctness of the outputs.

1091
00:52:40,930 --> 00:52:43,330
It's an open research
question, what that

1092
00:52:43,330 --> 00:52:45,020
means for a video generation.

1093
00:52:45,020 --> 00:52:47,160
How do you verify the
correctness of a video?

1094
00:52:47,160 --> 00:52:49,990

1095
00:52:49,990 --> 00:52:53,240
Very lastly, we have
native generation.

1096
00:52:53,240 --> 00:52:59,030
So recent large language
models are natively multimodal.

1097
00:52:59,030 --> 00:53:00,860
They can generate texts.

1098
00:53:00,860 --> 00:53:02,390
They can do image understanding.

1099
00:53:02,390 --> 00:53:04,250
They can do video understanding.

1100
00:53:04,250 --> 00:53:07,780
Some can even do
image generation.

1101
00:53:07,780 --> 00:53:11,500
So it's an interesting question
as to whether video generation

1102
00:53:11,500 --> 00:53:16,450
would also benefit from being
thrown into this native mix.

1103
00:53:16,450 --> 00:53:21,820
And if so, there are
interesting questions around how

1104
00:53:21,820 --> 00:53:23,240
you would train such a thing?

1105
00:53:23,240 --> 00:53:25,740
I've just talked you through
how flow matching seems to work

1106
00:53:25,740 --> 00:53:27,110
the best for video generation.

1107
00:53:27,110 --> 00:53:30,440
Is there a way that you can have
multiple learning objectives?

1108
00:53:30,440 --> 00:53:33,610
Do you need to unify
the learning objectives?

1109
00:53:33,610 --> 00:53:36,010
Things like that.

1110
00:53:36,010 --> 00:53:41,230
So very lastly,
it was a huge team

1111
00:53:41,230 --> 00:53:45,160
that worked on
Movie Gen. These are

1112
00:53:45,160 --> 00:53:46,513
a bunch of amazing researchers.

1113
00:53:46,513 --> 00:53:47,930
I had so much fun
on this project.

1114
00:53:47,930 --> 00:53:48,950
Learned so much.

1115
00:53:48,950 --> 00:53:51,970
There are loads of
good friends here.

1116
00:53:51,970 --> 00:53:56,140
So yeah, shout-outs
to all of them.

1117
00:53:56,140 --> 00:53:59,890
So I'm going to leave
it on this slide.

1118
00:53:59,890 --> 00:54:04,088
But we can do questions
now if there are any.

1119
00:54:04,088 --> 00:54:07,364
[APPLAUSE]

1120
00:54:07,364 --> 00:54:10,180

1121
00:54:10,180 --> 00:54:15,160
I guess especially given
[INAUDIBLE] is so involved.

1122
00:54:15,160 --> 00:54:17,670
And a lot of these
types of architectures

1123
00:54:17,670 --> 00:54:20,400
are unit-based with
that kind of structures.

1124
00:54:20,400 --> 00:54:24,670
And that-- so the actual
diffusion policy, surprisingly,

1125
00:54:24,670 --> 00:54:26,460
like the ConvNet
architecture outperforms

1126
00:54:26,460 --> 00:54:29,730
the transformer architecture for
action diffusion for robotics.

1127
00:54:29,730 --> 00:54:33,480
And they make an
argument that it

1128
00:54:33,480 --> 00:54:36,000
has an inductive
bias about smoothing,

1129
00:54:36,000 --> 00:54:38,580
so there's more
consistency in the spatial

1130
00:54:38,580 --> 00:54:41,370
and the time domain and
all that great stuff.

1131
00:54:41,370 --> 00:54:43,240
What did you say back
to that, I guess?

1132
00:54:43,240 --> 00:54:46,770

1133
00:54:46,770 --> 00:54:49,780
So I haven't read
that particular paper,

1134
00:54:49,780 --> 00:54:52,590
but it does sound
like a familiar point,

1135
00:54:52,590 --> 00:54:57,690
I guess the whole theory around
architectural unification

1136
00:54:57,690 --> 00:55:01,980
is that we might have
these specialized

1137
00:55:01,980 --> 00:55:07,410
architecture like CNNs, and
they hold these inductive biases

1138
00:55:07,410 --> 00:55:11,770
around visual data,
like they prioritize

1139
00:55:11,770 --> 00:55:14,170
these local interactions
with the convolutional mask,

1140
00:55:14,170 --> 00:55:15,010
and so on.

1141
00:55:15,010 --> 00:55:18,370
And the going idea
has been that when

1142
00:55:18,370 --> 00:55:21,580
you're training at small scale,
having these inductive biases

1143
00:55:21,580 --> 00:55:22,445
helps.

1144
00:55:22,445 --> 00:55:24,070
But when you're
training at large scale

1145
00:55:24,070 --> 00:55:27,910
and you have enough data, you
can learn all of these yourself

1146
00:55:27,910 --> 00:55:33,070
with a transformer in a
less constrained setting.

1147
00:55:33,070 --> 00:55:35,860
We have found that
scaling transformers here

1148
00:55:35,860 --> 00:55:40,480
works better than scaling
these specialized architectures

1149
00:55:40,480 --> 00:55:41,810
for a few reasons.

1150
00:55:41,810 --> 00:55:44,860
I mean, it's not
even to say that I

1151
00:55:44,860 --> 00:55:47,680
don't think CNN's could scale.

1152
00:55:47,680 --> 00:55:50,690
It seems as though it's
easier to scale transformers.

1153
00:55:50,690 --> 00:55:52,240
It's more
straightforward to know

1154
00:55:52,240 --> 00:55:54,010
in which direction to scale.

1155
00:55:54,010 --> 00:55:57,760
All the infrastructure
already exists.

1156
00:55:57,760 --> 00:55:59,480
But it's a very big debate.

1157
00:55:59,480 --> 00:56:03,070
I'm not like calling it.

1158
00:56:03,070 --> 00:56:03,975
Hey.

1159
00:56:03,975 --> 00:56:07,870
Can this modeling approach be
used for 3D generation to video

1160
00:56:07,870 --> 00:56:11,250
games and stuff like that?

1161
00:56:11,250 --> 00:56:17,190
So if you go back
to the architecture,

1162
00:56:17,190 --> 00:56:20,940
it's totally
modality independent.

1163
00:56:20,940 --> 00:56:27,930
So for any-- this is
for videos and images.

1164
00:56:27,930 --> 00:56:32,010
The important thing here is
that we've turned a modality

1165
00:56:32,010 --> 00:56:33,900
into a sequence of tokens.

1166
00:56:33,900 --> 00:56:36,120
At this point, after
that, everything

1167
00:56:36,120 --> 00:56:37,620
that's happening
in the architecture

1168
00:56:37,620 --> 00:56:39,190
is modality independent.

1169
00:56:39,190 --> 00:56:42,870
So really, for any new
kind of data, all you need

1170
00:56:42,870 --> 00:56:47,580
is a way of turning it
into a sequence of tokens.

1171
00:56:47,580 --> 00:56:55,773
It may be more challenging
to encode that kind of data.

1172
00:56:55,773 --> 00:56:57,690
But as long as you can
encode it, in some way,

1173
00:56:57,690 --> 00:57:00,148
to a series of tokens, you can
use the exact same approach.

1174
00:57:00,148 --> 00:57:04,860

1175
00:57:04,860 --> 00:57:07,070
We have a lot of
questions online as well.

1176
00:57:07,070 --> 00:57:11,080
So I can read a few
of those right now.

1177
00:57:11,080 --> 00:57:14,380
So one is, so 16 seconds seems
like the longest videos you can

1178
00:57:14,380 --> 00:57:15,890
effectively generate right now.

1179
00:57:15,890 --> 00:57:18,880
What are the main obstacles
or things keeping us

1180
00:57:18,880 --> 00:57:20,750
from getting to
longer, for example,

1181
00:57:20,750 --> 00:57:25,780
real movie-length generations?

1182
00:57:25,780 --> 00:57:29,960
So the main issue is
a computational one.

1183
00:57:29,960 --> 00:57:32,570
Well, there are so many
different answers to this.

1184
00:57:32,570 --> 00:57:35,440
If you're thinking from
the Movie Gen set up,

1185
00:57:35,440 --> 00:57:39,040
the issue is sequence length.

1186
00:57:39,040 --> 00:57:42,080
Given the level of
compression that we had,

1187
00:57:42,080 --> 00:57:45,310
73K was pretty much the longest
sequence length we could

1188
00:57:45,310 --> 00:57:47,470
feasibly train at.

1189
00:57:47,470 --> 00:57:50,000
If you want to train
on 32 second videos,

1190
00:57:50,000 --> 00:57:51,700
that's going to double.

1191
00:57:51,700 --> 00:57:53,690
There are multiple
ways around this.

1192
00:57:53,690 --> 00:57:56,830
You could train an encoder
with far more compression,

1193
00:57:56,830 --> 00:58:00,070
and then you could get
to videos that long.

1194
00:58:00,070 --> 00:58:06,110
There's another question
about learning objective.

1195
00:58:06,110 --> 00:58:08,653
Here, we generate
everything at once.

1196
00:58:08,653 --> 00:58:10,070
There are lots of
papers out there

1197
00:58:10,070 --> 00:58:14,010
that will iteratively generate
videos along the temporal axis.

1198
00:58:14,010 --> 00:58:16,280
You will generate a
chunk and then generate

1199
00:58:16,280 --> 00:58:18,440
a new chunk conditioned
on the previous trunk,

1200
00:58:18,440 --> 00:58:21,950
like next token prediction.

1201
00:58:21,950 --> 00:58:24,350
So I don't think
there's necessarily--

1202
00:58:24,350 --> 00:58:25,743
there are lots of
ways around it,

1203
00:58:25,743 --> 00:58:27,410
and there are lots
of interesting papers

1204
00:58:27,410 --> 00:58:29,630
that generate infinite
length videos using

1205
00:58:29,630 --> 00:58:31,446
these iterative processes.

1206
00:58:31,446 --> 00:58:36,650

1207
00:58:36,650 --> 00:58:38,750
Let me see.

1208
00:58:38,750 --> 00:58:42,320
There's some questions on
synthetic data compute.

1209
00:58:42,320 --> 00:58:44,210
So someone says, Meta
is a rare example

1210
00:58:44,210 --> 00:58:47,570
of having an abundance
of training data.

1211
00:58:47,570 --> 00:58:50,150
What if you run
out of video then?

1212
00:58:50,150 --> 00:58:51,480
What's next, I guess?

1213
00:58:51,480 --> 00:58:56,180

1214
00:58:56,180 --> 00:58:59,060
I really how to answer that.

1215
00:58:59,060 --> 00:59:02,770
If we run out of video--

1216
00:59:02,770 --> 00:59:04,020
look, I think there's--

1217
00:59:04,020 --> 00:59:08,950

1218
00:59:08,950 --> 00:59:11,860
I think that there's a
lot of interesting work

1219
00:59:11,860 --> 00:59:17,090
to be done on improving
the data filtration steps.

1220
00:59:17,090 --> 00:59:21,940
So in this slide,
we were optimizing

1221
00:59:21,940 --> 00:59:23,335
this for high precision.

1222
00:59:23,335 --> 00:59:25,210
So making sure that all
the videos at the end

1223
00:59:25,210 --> 00:59:26,920
were very high quality.

1224
00:59:26,920 --> 00:59:29,480
Recall was sacrificed
throughout this.

1225
00:59:29,480 --> 00:59:31,960
These are very
computer vision terms.

1226
00:59:31,960 --> 00:59:34,990
But we would have lost a lot
of good data in this very

1227
00:59:34,990 --> 00:59:36,910
complex pipeline here.

1228
00:59:36,910 --> 00:59:40,120
One way forward for
getting more data

1229
00:59:40,120 --> 00:59:42,250
is moving to smarter
ways of doing

1230
00:59:42,250 --> 00:59:45,470
this process, maybe completely
language model-based,

1231
00:59:45,470 --> 00:59:46,050
for example.

1232
00:59:46,050 --> 00:59:49,930

1233
00:59:49,930 --> 00:59:53,260
I think that's one answer.

1234
00:59:53,260 --> 00:59:54,770
A question about compute.

1235
00:59:54,770 --> 00:59:56,300
This is more general, I guess.

1236
00:59:56,300 --> 00:59:59,290
How can academic researchers
contribute to video generation

1237
00:59:59,290 --> 01:00:02,305
without having access
to thousands of GPUs?

1238
01:00:02,305 --> 01:00:06,800

1239
01:00:06,800 --> 01:00:09,080
It's obviously very
tough to do this level

1240
01:00:09,080 --> 01:00:14,690
of pre-training outside
of industry labs.

1241
01:00:14,690 --> 01:00:17,510
But throughout this paper, we've
used a bunch of innovations

1242
01:00:17,510 --> 01:00:20,510
that came from academia.

1243
01:00:20,510 --> 01:00:23,120
For example, a lot of
the flow matching work

1244
01:00:23,120 --> 01:00:24,630
was done at universities.

1245
01:00:24,630 --> 01:00:26,930
The main paper that we
take inspiration from

1246
01:00:26,930 --> 01:00:29,720
did come from Meta.

1247
01:00:29,720 --> 01:00:32,970
But this kind of research
can be done at small scale,

1248
01:00:32,970 --> 01:00:36,890
and then all of us
can learn from it.

1249
01:00:36,890 --> 01:00:39,445
The pre-training is very tough.

1250
01:00:39,445 --> 01:00:45,770

1251
01:00:45,770 --> 01:00:52,470
I think the
pre-training is tough.

1252
01:00:52,470 --> 01:00:55,880
But things like
learning objectives,

1253
01:00:55,880 --> 01:00:57,380
I think post-training
schemes, we've

1254
01:00:57,380 --> 01:01:00,840
seen a lot of great work coming
out of academia for that.

1255
01:01:00,840 --> 01:01:03,820

1256
01:01:03,820 --> 01:01:07,300
We've got a few questions,
all related to text, I guess.

1257
01:01:07,300 --> 01:01:10,000
Some folks are
saying, there's a lot

1258
01:01:10,000 --> 01:01:14,230
of work of cleaning and
processing video data.

1259
01:01:14,230 --> 01:01:18,410
How do you make sure that
the actual text, for example,

1260
01:01:18,410 --> 01:01:22,973
the Llama 3 generated captions,
are high quality and complete?

1261
01:01:22,973 --> 01:01:26,680

1262
01:01:26,680 --> 01:01:29,650
Good question.

1263
01:01:29,650 --> 01:01:32,740
We put a lot of work into
training this Llama 3

1264
01:01:32,740 --> 01:01:34,370
captioner, basically.

1265
01:01:34,370 --> 01:01:37,360
So this is a
video-conditioned Llama model.

1266
01:01:37,360 --> 01:01:40,810
This went through its
own large scale training

1267
01:01:40,810 --> 01:01:43,150
in order to generate
good-looking captions that were

1268
01:01:43,150 --> 01:01:46,600
aligned with what we wanted.

1269
01:01:46,600 --> 01:01:50,560
But certainly, there's a lot
of room for improvement there.

1270
01:01:50,560 --> 01:01:55,130
These captions are not as good
as human-written captions.

1271
01:01:55,130 --> 01:01:57,390
There are a lot of
architectural reasons for that.

1272
01:01:57,390 --> 01:02:02,870
A lot of these video-conditioned
language models cannot see

1273
01:02:02,870 --> 01:02:03,690
the whole video.

1274
01:02:03,690 --> 01:02:07,010
If it's a 16 second
video, it's 16 FPS.

1275
01:02:07,010 --> 01:02:11,690
Often, it's far too
much video for the model

1276
01:02:11,690 --> 01:02:12,810
to be conditioned on.

1277
01:02:12,810 --> 01:02:15,410
So often, with a lot of
these open source models,

1278
01:02:15,410 --> 01:02:18,080
not only Llama, but
Gemma and so on,

1279
01:02:18,080 --> 01:02:20,810
you have to subsample frames.

1280
01:02:20,810 --> 01:02:22,637
And so you're
blocking the model--

1281
01:02:22,637 --> 01:02:24,720
the language model from
seeing a lot of the video.

1282
01:02:24,720 --> 01:02:27,600
It's going to lead to some
issues, some missed things.

1283
01:02:27,600 --> 01:02:30,560
So we do our best by
training and post-training

1284
01:02:30,560 --> 01:02:33,660
a captioning model, doing a
bunch of evaluations on it.

1285
01:02:33,660 --> 01:02:35,940
But that's definitely
something that can be improved.

1286
01:02:35,940 --> 01:02:38,310
And there's a bunch of
really cool results out there

1287
01:02:38,310 --> 01:02:39,685
from the text-to-image
community,

1288
01:02:39,685 --> 01:02:41,520
showing that if you
improve captions,

1289
01:02:41,520 --> 01:02:44,040
your image quality gets better.

1290
01:02:44,040 --> 01:02:48,620
It's not entirely clear why,
but that keeps on happening.

1291
01:02:48,620 --> 01:02:49,940
Makes sense.

1292
01:02:49,940 --> 01:02:51,440
Someone asked a
related thing, which

1293
01:02:51,440 --> 01:02:54,850
is how much role or importance
does the text or prompt encoder

1294
01:02:54,850 --> 01:02:56,110
play?

1295
01:02:56,110 --> 01:02:58,900
I think there were some image
generation works, which showed

1296
01:02:58,900 --> 01:03:00,860
replacing the text encoder.

1297
01:03:00,860 --> 01:03:03,920
I think it was from CLIP to T5.

1298
01:03:03,920 --> 01:03:05,780
Really helped
improve performance.

1299
01:03:05,780 --> 01:03:10,730
Did you guys play around with
several different text encoders?

1300
01:03:10,730 --> 01:03:14,260
So this particular
series of text encodings

1301
01:03:14,260 --> 01:03:19,060
was precedent in our team.

1302
01:03:19,060 --> 01:03:21,760
We took motivation
from a recent state

1303
01:03:21,760 --> 01:03:24,250
of the art text-to-image
paper that used

1304
01:03:24,250 --> 01:03:25,850
this series of text encodings.

1305
01:03:25,850 --> 01:03:29,560
But it's worth pointing out
here that it is quite strange

1306
01:03:29,560 --> 01:03:32,260
what we've done here.

1307
01:03:32,260 --> 01:03:35,350
You would think it would
be intuitive that you

1308
01:03:35,350 --> 01:03:39,010
want your best possible
text representation here.

1309
01:03:39,010 --> 01:03:40,573
All of these text
representations

1310
01:03:40,573 --> 01:03:41,990
are nowhere near
state of the art.

1311
01:03:41,990 --> 01:03:45,940
They're not Llama,
they're not GPT.

1312
01:03:45,940 --> 01:03:49,240
There's been a few works and
empirical findings showing

1313
01:03:49,240 --> 01:03:53,000
that in this setup, at
least, decoder-only text

1314
01:03:53,000 --> 01:03:55,660
representations don't work
as well for some reason.

1315
01:03:55,660 --> 01:03:58,490

1316
01:03:58,490 --> 01:04:02,690
Some reasons for that could
be or have hypothesized

1317
01:04:02,690 --> 01:04:05,720
that you need a text
representation that is more

1318
01:04:05,720 --> 01:04:07,567
aligned with the media space.

1319
01:04:07,567 --> 01:04:09,650
That's why a lot of people
you'll see conditioning

1320
01:04:09,650 --> 01:04:13,670
on CLIP, which is what we do.

1321
01:04:13,670 --> 01:04:16,020
So a bunch of cool
work to do here.

1322
01:04:16,020 --> 01:04:20,630
We didn't ablate in
this project, though.

1323
01:04:20,630 --> 01:04:23,600
I have a question followed
up on the text part.

1324
01:04:23,600 --> 01:04:27,510
How well would this do
with very detailed prompts?

1325
01:04:27,510 --> 01:04:31,400
Like I want to move a video
about this person wearing

1326
01:04:31,400 --> 01:04:35,510
this specific color, and here's
what happens later on, and then

1327
01:04:35,510 --> 01:04:37,040
a very detailed script.

1328
01:04:37,040 --> 01:04:42,020
Rather than just a video
of some penguins, I guess.

1329
01:04:42,020 --> 01:04:46,760
We definitely did observe
that the model can

1330
01:04:46,760 --> 01:04:51,600
do these sequential actions,
but not always completely

1331
01:04:51,600 --> 01:04:54,210
accurately.

1332
01:04:54,210 --> 01:04:56,880
Some of that might be
issues in the captioning

1333
01:04:56,880 --> 01:05:00,720
of the pre-training data, like
are you accurately captioning

1334
01:05:00,720 --> 01:05:03,120
all of these things happening?

1335
01:05:03,120 --> 01:05:05,040
But that will be
one of the places

1336
01:05:05,040 --> 01:05:08,100
where it struggles if you were
to detail three or four things

1337
01:05:08,100 --> 01:05:11,220
happening in sequence.

1338
01:05:11,220 --> 01:05:14,190
We have one question, which
is, could you somehow hard code

1339
01:05:14,190 --> 01:05:16,290
in some priors
related to physics

1340
01:05:16,290 --> 01:05:20,190
and real-world common sense to
improve the realism and accuracy

1341
01:05:20,190 --> 01:05:21,400
of generated videos?

1342
01:05:21,400 --> 01:05:24,870
I guess that might be related to
that video of the car splitting

1343
01:05:24,870 --> 01:05:25,775
and all of that.

1344
01:05:25,775 --> 01:05:35,190

1345
01:05:35,190 --> 01:05:42,570
So in a way, it's like the
antithesis of the things

1346
01:05:42,570 --> 01:05:44,850
that we were trying
here, of removing

1347
01:05:44,850 --> 01:05:49,850
all of the inductive biases and
just scaling, compute, and data.

1348
01:05:49,850 --> 01:05:53,190
But I do think those are
interesting things to try.

1349
01:05:53,190 --> 01:05:57,140
Simply because maybe
if you are trying

1350
01:05:57,140 --> 01:05:58,620
to learn the laws
of physics better

1351
01:05:58,620 --> 01:06:00,980
in just random videos
from a large pool

1352
01:06:00,980 --> 01:06:04,820
aren't maybe the best
thing to use there.

1353
01:06:04,820 --> 01:06:08,780
There are other cool works
that have been released,

1354
01:06:08,780 --> 01:06:11,630
where they're trained entirely
on video game data, things

1355
01:06:11,630 --> 01:06:12,175
like that.

1356
01:06:12,175 --> 01:06:16,400

1357
01:06:16,400 --> 01:06:18,980
I think encoding
some of priors would

1358
01:06:18,980 --> 01:06:20,990
be cool because there
are certain things

1359
01:06:20,990 --> 01:06:23,780
about the natural world
that we do know for sure.

1360
01:06:23,780 --> 01:06:27,015
Something like computer
vision principles, and so on.

1361
01:06:27,015 --> 01:06:28,390
But yeah, we didn't
do that here.

1362
01:06:28,390 --> 01:06:31,190
Do you know how to
possibly encode those?

1363
01:06:31,190 --> 01:06:32,360
Yeah.

1364
01:06:32,360 --> 01:06:34,170
That's like a super
open question.

1365
01:06:34,170 --> 01:06:35,250
Super open question.

1366
01:06:35,250 --> 01:06:36,320
Makes sense.

1367
01:06:36,320 --> 01:06:37,760
We've got a couple
others online,

1368
01:06:37,760 --> 01:06:39,830
and then we can go
back to in-person.

1369
01:06:39,830 --> 01:06:43,100
One's more on deepfakes
and malicious use

1370
01:06:43,100 --> 01:06:45,120
of video generation models.

1371
01:06:45,120 --> 01:06:49,950
Is there any work on things like
watermarking to deal with that?

1372
01:06:49,950 --> 01:06:52,516
There's definitely a bunch
of work on watermarking.

1373
01:06:52,516 --> 01:06:58,120

1374
01:06:58,120 --> 01:07:00,450
There's definitely a bunch
of work on watermarking

1375
01:07:00,450 --> 01:07:02,500
coming from a few groups.

1376
01:07:02,500 --> 01:07:06,210
DeepMind has released a few
interesting papers on it.

1377
01:07:06,210 --> 01:07:08,850
NASA has a team working
on this as well.

1378
01:07:08,850 --> 01:07:10,805
So yeah, very important
work to be done.

1379
01:07:10,805 --> 01:07:16,350

1380
01:07:16,350 --> 01:07:18,090
I'll read one, and
then we can go back

1381
01:07:18,090 --> 01:07:20,010
to some in-person questions.

1382
01:07:20,010 --> 01:07:22,590
One's about GANs, actually.

1383
01:07:22,590 --> 01:07:25,080
I've noticed some older models
have a GAN discriminator

1384
01:07:25,080 --> 01:07:28,300
before or in the decoder
for temporal coherence.

1385
01:07:28,300 --> 01:07:33,720
Is this still useful or does
scale overcome this need?

1386
01:07:33,720 --> 01:07:37,260
They mean in the VAE decoder?

1387
01:07:37,260 --> 01:07:39,060
I think so.

1388
01:07:39,060 --> 01:07:44,190
So we also have a GAN
discriminator there.

1389
01:07:44,190 --> 01:07:49,760
It's not necessarily for
temporal consistency.

1390
01:07:49,760 --> 01:07:52,700
That might be one
of the outcomes.

1391
01:07:52,700 --> 01:07:56,270
There's a historical
precedent for this.

1392
01:07:56,270 --> 01:07:59,190
When you are training these--

1393
01:07:59,190 --> 01:07:59,690
where is it?

1394
01:07:59,690 --> 01:08:03,633
When you're training
these VAEs, folk

1395
01:08:03,633 --> 01:08:06,050
found-- it was the Stable
Diffusion researchers, actually,

1396
01:08:06,050 --> 01:08:09,170
while they were still
at college in Germany.

1397
01:08:09,170 --> 01:08:15,410
They published this paper called
VQGAN back in 2021, I think.

1398
01:08:15,410 --> 01:08:17,450
And they showed
that normally how

1399
01:08:17,450 --> 01:08:19,160
you train these VAEs
is that you just

1400
01:08:19,160 --> 01:08:22,700
use L1 losses between
the input and the output.

1401
01:08:22,700 --> 01:08:25,550
But they found that the amount
of compression you could do

1402
01:08:25,550 --> 01:08:27,540
was very limited when
you were doing that.

1403
01:08:27,540 --> 01:08:31,279
So what they started doing was
adding some adversarial losses

1404
01:08:31,279 --> 01:08:33,229
from the GAN literature.

1405
01:08:33,229 --> 01:08:36,770
What this does is
it tells the VAE

1406
01:08:36,770 --> 01:08:41,810
that I don't have to decode
exactly what was given to me.

1407
01:08:41,810 --> 01:08:44,618
Because the GAN losses,
they're not L1 losses.

1408
01:08:44,618 --> 01:08:45,910
They're not pixel-level losses.

1409
01:08:45,910 --> 01:08:48,180
These losses are
just how well the GAN

1410
01:08:48,180 --> 01:08:51,340
can tell if it's a real
image or a fake one.

1411
01:08:51,340 --> 01:08:55,979
So when folks started training
VAEs with these losses,

1412
01:08:55,979 --> 01:09:00,550
the outputs were given
a bit more freedom.

1413
01:09:00,550 --> 01:09:02,069
So the model could
generate a range

1414
01:09:02,069 --> 01:09:05,069
of things for a certain
input and it's not

1415
01:09:05,069 --> 01:09:07,779
getting penalized from the
loss as long as they look real.

1416
01:09:07,779 --> 01:09:11,050
And I realize there's a
bunch of detail there.

1417
01:09:11,050 --> 01:09:12,870
But basically, by
adding these losses,

1418
01:09:12,870 --> 01:09:17,399
we were able to get another
2x level of compression.

1419
01:09:17,399 --> 01:09:20,710
So we are also using
adversarial losses in the VAE

1420
01:09:20,710 --> 01:09:23,130
if that was their question.

1421
01:09:23,130 --> 01:09:25,090
Someone added an interesting
question just now,

1422
01:09:25,090 --> 01:09:29,700
which is, how might you
make sure all the videos

1423
01:09:29,700 --> 01:09:31,500
in your training data are real?

1424
01:09:31,500 --> 01:09:32,890
What if some of them are fake?

1425
01:09:32,890 --> 01:09:34,920
I guess that's
related to some work

1426
01:09:34,920 --> 01:09:37,330
nowadays in language models.

1427
01:09:37,330 --> 01:09:40,380
But once more and
more training data

1428
01:09:40,380 --> 01:09:44,210
becomes synthetically generated,
does that lead to any issues,

1429
01:09:44,210 --> 01:09:45,910
I guess?

1430
01:09:45,910 --> 01:09:50,290
Definitely this idea of data set
poisoning, it's often called,

1431
01:09:50,290 --> 01:09:53,109
is a problem.

1432
01:09:53,109 --> 01:09:57,380
I think there are a couple
interesting answers to this.

1433
01:09:57,380 --> 01:10:02,230
Firstly, in our pre-training
data, if it's low quality,

1434
01:10:02,230 --> 01:10:03,760
then we don't want
to train on it.

1435
01:10:03,760 --> 01:10:06,800
And hopefully, we would
find it and get rid of it.

1436
01:10:06,800 --> 01:10:11,350
But training on generated data
is not always a bad thing.

1437
01:10:11,350 --> 01:10:15,070
Most contemporary post-training
approaches for language models

1438
01:10:15,070 --> 01:10:17,660
are based upon training
on generated data.

1439
01:10:17,660 --> 01:10:20,330
You train on generations
from the model itself.

1440
01:10:20,330 --> 01:10:24,550
So it's not like training on
generated data is always bad.

1441
01:10:24,550 --> 01:10:27,400
If there's some really
bad generated videos

1442
01:10:27,400 --> 01:10:32,170
in the pre-training data, we
do want to get rid of those.

1443
01:10:32,170 --> 01:10:33,525
But it's not always bad.

1444
01:10:33,525 --> 01:10:36,040

1445
01:10:36,040 --> 01:10:39,690
We've got time for a couple
more in-person questions.

1446
01:10:39,690 --> 01:10:41,340
Can you share-- I
mean, I understand

1447
01:10:41,340 --> 01:10:43,320
that it will be
confidential-- but just to get

1448
01:10:43,320 --> 01:10:46,080
a sense of [? data ?]
structure that it took

1449
01:10:46,080 --> 01:10:49,030
to do the training as
well as a different time,

1450
01:10:49,030 --> 01:10:53,290
how big a company is or hosting
a single instance of the model?

1451
01:10:53,290 --> 01:10:57,880
How would you have a GPU,
GPUs to run every request?

1452
01:10:57,880 --> 01:11:00,425
Or if you can share those
just to get a sense of.

1453
01:11:00,425 --> 01:11:03,210

1454
01:11:03,210 --> 01:11:06,180
In the paper, we
included full details

1455
01:11:06,180 --> 01:11:08,130
of the training infrastructure.

1456
01:11:08,130 --> 01:11:08,980
That will be there.

1457
01:11:08,980 --> 01:11:14,910
I don't think we included any
details about the inference

1458
01:11:14,910 --> 01:11:18,300
of the structure of that.

1459
01:11:18,300 --> 01:11:20,590
There are lots of details
on the infra in the paper.

1460
01:11:20,590 --> 01:11:22,007
I would recommend
you to go there.

1461
01:11:22,007 --> 01:11:26,100

1462
01:11:26,100 --> 01:11:28,907
Any other questions?

1463
01:11:28,907 --> 01:11:30,990
I don't know if I missed
that, but for the videos,

1464
01:11:30,990 --> 01:11:34,630
you generated, do
they come with audio?

1465
01:11:34,630 --> 01:11:35,130
Yeah.

1466
01:11:35,130 --> 01:11:40,040
So this text-to-video
model that I've shown here,

1467
01:11:40,040 --> 01:11:41,360
it generates video.

1468
01:11:41,360 --> 01:11:47,830
But with the publication,
we released--

1469
01:11:47,830 --> 01:11:51,670
well, published about a--

1470
01:11:51,670 --> 01:11:53,540
where is it?

1471
01:11:53,540 --> 01:11:55,790
Actually, no, we're not going
over that problem again.

1472
01:11:55,790 --> 01:11:56,750
There's no audio.

1473
01:11:56,750 --> 01:11:59,320
[INAUDIBLE]

1474
01:11:59,320 --> 01:12:00,940
Yes.

1475
01:12:00,940 --> 01:12:06,430
So there were-- there was a
separate Movie Gen audio model

1476
01:12:06,430 --> 01:12:10,040
that we trained that will add
audio to a generated video.

1477
01:12:10,040 --> 01:12:14,480
[INAUDIBLE] the video
has two separate models,

1478
01:12:14,480 --> 01:12:19,690
and the audio is another
level of complexity, where

1479
01:12:19,690 --> 01:12:21,440
there are multiple tracks.

1480
01:12:21,440 --> 01:12:26,112
And if you want to train on MIDI
files for MP3, like [INAUDIBLE].

1481
01:12:26,112 --> 01:12:29,980

1482
01:12:29,980 --> 01:12:35,970
So what is the current progress
of this audio generation?

1483
01:12:35,970 --> 01:12:40,440
So we have a really
great audio research

1484
01:12:40,440 --> 01:12:43,650
team that works on this.

1485
01:12:43,650 --> 01:12:45,880
One very nice thing
to do, I think,

1486
01:12:45,880 --> 01:12:50,880
would be to generate everything
at once, like video and audio.

1487
01:12:50,880 --> 01:12:53,260
The two modalities
are very correlated.

1488
01:12:53,260 --> 01:12:54,760
There's a lot of
shared information.

1489
01:12:54,760 --> 01:12:58,410
So theoretically,
both modalities

1490
01:12:58,410 --> 01:13:00,820
should benefit from
being trained together.

1491
01:13:00,820 --> 01:13:02,520
The audio even encodes
some information

1492
01:13:02,520 --> 01:13:06,480
about videos that are
not present in the video.

1493
01:13:06,480 --> 01:13:08,880
The issue is one of data.

1494
01:13:08,880 --> 01:13:11,260
It's very hard to get
high-quality video data.

1495
01:13:11,260 --> 01:13:13,200
It's even harder to
get high-quality video

1496
01:13:13,200 --> 01:13:16,410
with good audio data.

1497
01:13:16,410 --> 01:13:18,870
So that's part of the
reason why we didn't

1498
01:13:18,870 --> 01:13:19,970
do that for this project.

1499
01:13:19,970 --> 01:13:23,090

1500
01:13:23,090 --> 01:13:23,590
Awesome.

1501
01:13:23,590 --> 01:13:26,640
Thanks so much, Andrew, for
the very insightful talk

1502
01:13:26,640 --> 01:13:28,200
and answering all our questions.

1503
